var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"models/#Using-different-models","page":"Using different models","text":"Any MLJ Deterministic regressor can be the base model of a Forecaster. To change the learner you swap the model and nothing else: the features, the strategy and the data stay as they are. This page fits six learners from six packages to the same panel of 1399 monthly series from the M3 competition, each as one global model with the same specification and its package's default hyperparameters, apart from one XGBoost setting explained in The models. It scores them on the M3 holdout next to two baselines and published results.\n\nThe code below runs top to bottom as one script, and the outputs shown are from that run. Besides MachineLearningForecast it uses the standard libraries Downloads, Dates, Statistics and Printf, plus these packages, none of which is a dependency of MachineLearningForecast: ZipArchives, EvoTrees, MLJDecisionTreeInterface, MLJLinearModels, MLJXGBoostInterface, LightGBM and NearestNeighborModels. Add them to your environment with Pkg.add. MachineLearningForecast is not registered yet, so add it by URL: Pkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/Akai01/MachineLearningForecast.jl\").","title":"Using different models"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-data","page":"Using different models","text":"The series are the 1428 monthly series of the M3 competition (Makridakis, S. and Hibon, M. (2000), \"The M3-Competition: results, conclusions and implications\", International Journal of Forecasting 16(4), 451–476). The code downloads them from the Monash Time Series Forecasting Archive (Godahewa, R., Bergmeir, C., Webb, G. I., Hyndman, R. J. and Montero-Manso, P. (2021), \"Monash Time Series Forecasting Archive\", NeurIPS Track on Datasets and Benchmarks): the \"M3 Monthly Dataset\" on Zenodo, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.4656298, licensed under CC BY 4.0.\n\nThe zip file holds one file in the archive's .tsf format: comment and @attribute lines, then, after @data, one series per line as name:start:values, for example T1:1990-01-01 00-00-00:2640,2640,2160,....\n\nusing Downloads, ZipArchives, Dates\n\nurl = \"https://zenodo.org/api/records/4656298/files/m3_monthly_dataset.zip/content\"\narchive = ZipReader(take!(Downloads.download(url, IOBuffer())))\ntsf = zip_readentry(archive, \"m3_monthly_dataset.tsf\", String)\n\nfunction parse_series(line)\n    name, start, values = split(line, ':')\n    return (id = String(name), start = Date(start[1:10]),\n            y = parse.(Float64, split(values, ',')))\nend\n\nlines = eachline(IOBuffer(split(tsf, \"@data\")[2]))\nseries = [parse_series(l) for l in lines if !isempty(l)]\ndated = filter(s -> s.start != Date(1900, 1, 1), series)\nprintln(length(series), \" series, \", length(dated), \" with a start date\")\n\n1428 series, 1399 with a start date\n\nThe file's header notes that the start dates of the last 29 series are unknown and were set to 1900-01-01. The feature set below reads the month from each timestamp, so those series would get made-up months: they are left out, which leaves 1399 series.","title":"The data"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-holdout","page":"Using different models","text":"The evaluation follows the M3 protocol: the last 18 months of each series are held out, and everything before them is the series' training history. All the steps below use the training history only; the held-out months are used only to score the forecasts.\n\nThe M3 series span different calendar years (they end in 73 different months, from 1864 to 2005), so no single cut date would hold out each series' own last 18 months. backtest with windows = 1 cuts each series at its own end instead, and with horizon = 18 it holds out exactly the M3 months. train and test split a series the same way for the two baselines below.\n\nusing Statistics\n\nh = 18\ntrain(s) = s.y[1:(end - h)]\ntest(s) = s.y[(end - h + 1):end]","title":"The holdout"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-transform","page":"Using different models","text":"One global model fits all series with one loss, so the target has to mean the same thing in every series. M3 series differ in level, many trend, and many are seasonal. Tree models, such as boosted trees and random forests, predict averages of the targets they were trained on, so they cannot extrapolate beyond the levels they saw. Dividing each series by its whole-history mean does not fix that: a series that trends up ends far from its old mean, and the model pulls its forecasts back towards old levels. The transform below models each month relative to the months just before it instead, and rebuilds the forecasts from each series' own last year.\n\nFor each series, from its training history only, three built-in TargetTransforms are applied in turn:\n\nLevel, LastScale(12). Divide the series by m, the mean of its last 12 values, so every series is near 1 at its forecast origin.\nSeasonal adjustment, Deseasonalize(12). The series is seasonal if its autocorrelation at lag 12, r_12, passes the 90% test that the M4 competition's benchmarks use: r_12  1645 sqrt(1 + 2 sum_k=1^11 r_k^2)  n on n ge 36 points (every M3 training history has at least 48). A seasonal series gets the classical multiplicative indices: each value is divided by its centred 2×12 moving average, these ratios are averaged by position in the year, and the 12 averages are normalised to mean 1. A non-seasonal series gets indices of 1. Dividing by the indices gives the seasonally adjusted series x.\nLog ratio, LogRatio(12). z_t = log(x_t  barx_t), where barx_t is the mean of the 12 values before x_t: each month relative to the year before it. A ratio has no unit, so z does not depend on the level of step 1 and is on the same scale in every series. It is 12 values shorter than the history.\nInverse. Forecasts of z are turned back one month at a time: exp(hatz) times the mean of the previous 12 values of x, rebuilt ones included, then times that month's seasonal index and m.\n\nTransformChain applies the three in order and undoes them in reverse. Given as the transform of a Forecaster, it is fitted on each series' own training history, refitted in every backtest fold, and the forecasts come back on the original scale.\n\nPositions in the year are counted from each series' first month, so the forecast for month n + j of a history of length n gets the index at position mod1(n + j, 12). The log needs positive values: every M3 training history is positive, and LogRatio and Deseasonalize throw an error naming the series and the value otherwise. A series with zeros or negative values needs a shift first.\n\nThis setup was chosen on a validation window, the 18 months before the holdout, among several target transforms and feature sets, and was then run once on the holdout.\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast\n\nchain = TransformChain(LastScale(12), Deseasonalize(12), LogRatio(12))","title":"The transform"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-panel","page":"Using different models","text":"long puts series into one long-format table with columns unique_id, ds and y, each series whole, its held-out months included: backtest cuts them off. The transform drops the first 12 months of each training history, which have no year before them.\n\nlong(ss) = (unique_id = reduce(vcat, [fill(s.id, length(s.y)) for s in ss]),\n            ds = reduce(vcat, [s.start .+ Month.(0:(length(s.y) - 1)) for s in ss]),\n            y = reduce(vcat, [s.y for s in ss]))\npanel = long(dated)\nprintln(length(panel.y), \" rows\")\n\n165503 rows","title":"The panel"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#One-specification-for-every-learner","page":"Using different models","text":"Every learner gets the same FeatureSet (lags 1 to 12 of z, its 12-month rolling mean and the calendar month), the same Recursive strategy and the transform, with id = :unique_id to fit one model across the panel. The month comes from Calendar. On a panel, Fourier would mark the same month in every series as well, since it counts steps from the earliest series' first month, although these series start in different months (1155 in January, 198 in October, 46 in others).\n\nfeatures = FeatureSet(Lag.(1:12)..., RollingMean(12), Calendar(:month))\nspec(model) = Forecaster(model; features, strategy = Recursive(), freq = Month(1),\n                         id = :unique_id, transform = chain)","title":"One specification for every learner"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Scoring","page":"Using different models","text":"evaluate runs backtest with windows = 1 on a panel: it fits one model on every series' training history, forecasts 18 months for every series (each from its own last training month), and scores the forecasts with smape and mase on the original scale. The M3 benchmarks scale MASE by the seasonal naive error, so mase12 is Mase(12), and backtest passes it each series' own training values. backtest reports MASE averaged over the series and sMAPE over all forecast months, which is the same average over the series, since each has 18. sMAPE is shown in percent.\n\nnote: Why sMAPE\nThis page reports sMAPE only because the M3 competition did, so the results can be compared with published ones; it does not recommend it. Hyndman, R. J. and Koehler, A. B. (2006), \"Another look at measures of forecast accuracy\", International Journal of Forecasting 22(4), 679–688, found the measures used in the M3 competition inadequate in commonly occurring situations, such as values close to zero, and proposed MASE as the standard for comparing accuracy across series. MASE is reported alongside for that reason.\n\nThere are two baselines, scored the same way series by series. The seasonal naive forecast repeats each series' last 12 training months. Naive2, one of the M3 competition's benchmarks, repeats the last seasonally adjusted value and puts the season back. fit_transform with Deseasonalize(12) does the adjustment, with the same seasonal indices as step 2 of the transform, since they do not depend on the level, and untransform the inverse. Neither is exported, so the code imports them. A transform of your own that drops rows, as LogRatio and Difference do, declares them with dropped_rows, so that fit skips a series too short for it instead of failing on it.\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: fit_transform, untransform\n\nconst mase12 = Mase(12)\n\nfunction evaluate(model, data = panel)\n    t0 = time()\n    r = backtest(spec(model), data; horizon = h, windows = 1, metrics = (smape, mase12))\n    seconds = time() - t0\n    s, m = summarize(r).mean\n    return 100 * s, m, seconds\nend\n\nfunction scores(forecasts)\n    smapes = [smape(test(s), forecasts[s.id]) for s in dated]\n    mases = [mase12(test(s), forecasts[s.id]; y_train = train(s)) for s in dated]\n    return 100 * mean(smapes), mean(mases)\nend\n\nfunction naive2(y)\n    z, state = fit_transform(Deseasonalize(12), y)\n    return untransform(Deseasonalize(12), fill(z[end], h), state)\nend\n\nsnaive = Dict(s.id => repeat(train(s)[(end - 11):end], 2)[1:h] for s in dated)\nnaive2s = Dict(s.id => naive2(train(s)) for s in dated)","title":"Scoring"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#The-models","page":"Using different models","text":"Each model is used with its package's default hyperparameters, except XGBoost's base_score, set below. Where a model draws random numbers, its seed is fixed, so the results reproduce.\n\nModel Package Kind\nEvoTreeRegressor EvoTrees gradient-boosted trees\nRandomForestRegressor MLJDecisionTreeInterface (DecisionTree.jl) random forest\nLinearRegressor MLJLinearModels least squares\nXGBoostRegressor MLJXGBoostInterface (XGBoost.jl) gradient-boosted trees\nLGBMRegressor LightGBM gradient-boosted trees\nKNNRegressor NearestNeighborModels k nearest neighbours\n\nThe linear model is plain least squares, which has no hyperparameter. A penalised one such as RidgeRegressor needs its penalty chosen for the scale of the features, which would tune one learner and not the others. LightGBM does not export its MLJ model, so it is reached as LightGBM.MLJInterface.LGBMRegressor, and verbosity = -1 silences its training log.\n\nBoosted trees start from a constant prediction and add trees to correct it. LightGBM and EvoTrees start from the mean of the target, but MLJ's XGBoostRegressor starts from its base_score hyperparameter, which defaults to 0.5. The target z here is a log ratio centred near 0, so XGBoost is given base_score = 0.0, its natural centre, which puts it on the same footing as the other two. With the default 0.5, every XGBoost model first has to unlearn an offset of 0.5: a small learning rate or few rounds then leave part of it in every forecast. The setting was changed after a first run on the holdout, in which XGBoost with the default 0.5 scored 13.85 sMAPE and 0.860 MASE. Tuning the models measures the effect on two such configurations.\n\nusing EvoTrees, MLJDecisionTreeInterface, MLJLinearModels, MLJXGBoostInterface\nusing LightGBM, NearestNeighborModels\n\nmodels = [\n    EvoTreeRegressor(seed = 1),\n    RandomForestRegressor(rng = 1),\n    LinearRegressor(),\n    XGBoostRegressor(seed = 1, base_score = 0.0),\n    LightGBM.MLJInterface.LGBMRegressor(seed = 1, verbosity = -1),\n    KNNRegressor(),\n]","title":"The models"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Results","page":"Using different models","text":"Each model is evaluated twice, and the table shows the second run: the first call to a model includes Julia's compilation, which would otherwise dominate the time.\n\nusing Printf\n\n@printf(\"%-22s %8s %6s %8s\\n\", \"model\", \"sMAPE %\", \"MASE\", \"seconds\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %8.2f %6.3f %8s\\n\", \"seasonal naive\", scores(snaive)..., \"-\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %8.2f %6.3f %8s\\n\", \"Naive2\", scores(naive2s)..., \"-\")\nfor model in models\n    evaluate(model)  # the first call compiles; time the second\n    @printf(\"%-22s %8.2f %6.3f %8.1f\\n\", nameof(typeof(model)), evaluate(model)...)\nend\n\nmodel                   sMAPE %   MASE  seconds\nseasonal naive            17.26  1.146        -\nNaive2                    16.93  1.046        -\nEvoTreeRegressor          13.67  0.854      0.6\nRandomForestRegressor     13.66  0.855     11.6\nLinearRegressor           14.58  0.991      0.2\nXGBoostRegressor          13.78  0.858      0.5\nLGBMRegressor             13.71  0.856      0.5\nKNNRegressor              14.32  0.898      2.4\n\nAll six learners beat both baselines on both metrics: their sMAPE is 2.3 to 3.3 points below Naive2's, and every MASE is below 1, while both baselines' are above it. The four tree ensembles score 13.66 to 13.78, the nearest-neighbour model 14.32 and the linear model 14.58. The random forest took the longest to fit and forecast. Differences of a few hundredths between learners mean little here: step 1 of the transform cancels in step 3, so leaving it out changes z only by rounding, yet in a separate run it moved the sMAPE of EvoTrees, the random forest and LightGBM from 13.67, 13.66 and 13.71 to 13.64, 13.62 and 13.74.\n\nFor calibration, the table below lists every method with a monthly result in Table 14 of Oreshkin, B. N., Carpov, D., Chapados, N. and Bengio, Y. (2020), \"N-BEATS: Neural basis expansion analysis for interpretable time series forecasting\", International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2020). Those numbers are on all 1428 series and this page uses 1399, so the comparison is approximate. Naive2 scores 16.93 here against the published 16.91, so the scoring and the subset are close to the published setting.\n\nMethod sMAPE %\nNaive2 16.91\nARIMA (B-J automatic) 14.81\nComb S-H-D 14.48\nForecastPro 13.86\nTheta 13.85\nDOTM 13.74\nBaggedETS.BC 13.74\nEXP 13.43\nN-BEATS-G 13.19\nN-BEATS-I 13.15\nN-BEATS-I+G 13.11\n\nThe four tree ensembles land level with Theta, the winner of M3, ForecastPro, DOTM and bagged ETS. EXP, a later statistical method, is 0.2 to 0.35 ahead of them, and N-BEATS about 0.5 to 0.7. That does not show that the trees beat Theta: the margin over Theta is at most 0.19, there is no significance test, and the series differ. The nearest-neighbour and linear models are close to Comb S-H-D and ahead of ARIMA.\n\nTuning the models tunes each learner's hyperparameters on a validation window and scores the chosen configurations on this holdout.\n\nTested with Julia 1.12.5 and 1.10.12, each started with --threads=auto (20 threads), which gave the same scores; the times above are from Julia 1.12.5 and depend on the machine and the thread count. Run alone, download included, the whole script took about a minute on both versions and peaked at about 2.8 GiB of memory on Julia 1.12.5 and 5 GiB on 1.10.12. Package versions: MachineLearningForecast 0.1.0, EvoTrees 0.19.0, MLJDecisionTreeInterface 0.5.0, MLJLinearModels 0.10.4, MLJXGBoostInterface 0.3.13, LightGBM 2.2.2, NearestNeighborModels 0.2.3 and ZipArchives 2.6.1.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Next-steps","page":"Using different models","text":"These are default hyperparameters, base_score aside, on one feature set. To tune the hyperparameters of these six learners on a validation window, continue with Tuning the models. To search over models, hyperparameters, feature sets and strategies, scored on backtest error, see tune.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Tuning-the-models","page":"Tuning the models","text":"Using different models fits six learners with their packages' default hyperparameters, XGBoost's base_score aside. This page tunes the hyperparameters of each learner on a validation window, then scores the chosen configuration once on the same M3 holdout.\n\nThis page continues the session of Using different models: run that page's code first. The code below reuses its data, h, long, spec with its features and transform, evaluate and the list of models, and adds the standard library Random. It runs top to bottom after that page's code, and the outputs shown are from that run.","title":"Tuning the models"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#The-validation-window","page":"Tuning the models","text":"The tuning must not see the holdout, so it scores on the window before it. For each series, the validation history is y[1:end-36], the training history without its last 18 months, and the validation target is the 18 months that follow it. vpanel is the panel with the holdout cut off each series, so evaluate on it, with backtest and windows = 1, scores exactly this window, and the transform is fitted on the validation history only, as it was fitted on the training history before. 291 of the 1399 validation histories are shorter than 36 months (the shortest has 30), too short for the seasonality test, so Deseasonalize treats them as non-seasonal. validate returns the sMAPE and MASE.\n\nvdated = [(id = s.id, start = s.start, y = s.y[1:(end - h)]) for s in dated]\nvpanel = long(vdated)\nprintln(length(vpanel.y), \" rows\")\n\nvalidate(model) = evaluate(model, vpanel)[1:2]\n\n140321 rows\n\nThe split is time-ordered on purpose: the validation window comes after the training window, as the forecast period does in real use. If you tune the base model with MLJ's TunedModel, prefer TimeSeriesCV to CV(). Plain K-fold CV trains on rows that come after some of its test rows. For a stationary series, pure lag features and a model whose one-step residuals are not autocorrelated, this does not bias the estimate (Bergmeir, Hyndman & Koo 2018); it does for a trending or otherwise non-stationary series (Cerqueira, Torgo & Mozetič 2020) or when the model underfits and its residuals are autocorrelated. The result does not cover covariates or multi-step targets. TimeSeriesCV never trains on rows after its test rows on a single series, so it avoids these problems there. On a panel the pooled frame is ordered by series, not by time, so score on a validation window or use tune instead. The 18-step forecasts on this page, for a trending panel, are outside the Bergmeir, Hyndman & Koo result. The references are listed in Leakage and conventions.","title":"The validation window"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#The-search-spaces","page":"Tuning the models","text":"Every learner gets the same budget of 30 configurations, and configuration 1 is its model from the previous page. For the four tree ensembles, random_search draws the other 29 from the space below: each hyperparameter uniformly from its list, from a random number generator with a fixed seed, and a draw that repeats an earlier configuration is drawn again. The linear and nearest-neighbour spaces have exactly 30 points, so all of them are tried, in order. The linear one is least squares, then ridge with 15 penalties from 10^-8 to 10^-1 and lasso with 14 from 10^-8 to 10^-15, in half-decade steps. The nearest-neighbour one is ten values of K under three neighbour weightings. Each search pairs its configurations with a function that builds a model from one.\n\nusing Random\n\n# A repeat would spend budget on a known configuration.\nfunction random_search(model, space)\n    rng = MersenneTwister(2026)\n    configs = [NamedTuple{keys(space)}(getproperty.(Ref(model), keys(space)))]\n    while length(configs) < 30\n        c = map(v -> rand(rng, v), space)\n        c in configs || push!(configs, c)\n    end\n    return configs\nend\n\nevotrees = random_search(models[1],\n    (nrounds = [100, 200, 400, 800], eta = [0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2],\n     max_depth = [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8], L2 = [0.0, 1.0, 10.0], lambda = [0.0, 0.1, 1.0],\n     min_weight = [1.0, 10.0, 50.0], rowsample = [0.5, 0.8, 1.0],\n     colsample = [0.5, 0.8, 1.0]))\nforest = random_search(models[2],\n    (n_trees = [100, 300, 500], max_depth = [-1, 8, 12, 16],\n     min_samples_leaf = [1, 5, 20, 50], n_subfeatures = [-1, 0, 3, 4],\n     sampling_fraction = [0.5, 0.7, 1.0]))\nlinear = [(model = LinearRegressor, lambda = 0.0);\n          [(model = RidgeRegressor, lambda = l) for l in 10.0 .^ (-8:0.5:-1)];\n          [(model = LassoRegressor, lambda = l) for l in 10.0 .^ (-8:0.5:-1.5)]]\nxgboost = random_search(models[4],\n    (num_round = [100, 200, 400, 800], eta = [0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.3],\n     max_depth = [3, 4, 6, 8], subsample = [0.5, 0.8, 1.0],\n     colsample_bytree = [0.5, 0.8, 1.0], min_child_weight = [1.0, 10.0, 50.0]))\nlightgbm = random_search(models[5],\n    (num_iterations = [100, 200, 400, 800], learning_rate = [0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2],\n     num_leaves = [7, 15, 31, 63, 127], min_data_in_leaf = [5, 20, 50, 100, 200],\n     feature_fraction = [0.6, 0.8, 1.0], bagging_fraction = [0.6, 0.8, 1.0]))\nknn = [(K = k, weights = w) for w in (Uniform(), Inverse(), Dudani())\n       for k in (5, 10, 20, 30, 50, 75, 100, 150, 200, 300)]\n\nsearches = [\n    evotrees => c -> EvoTreeRegressor(; seed = 1, c...),\n    forest => c -> RandomForestRegressor(; rng = 1, c...),\n    linear => c -> c.model === LinearRegressor ? LinearRegressor() :\n                   c.model(lambda = c.lambda),\n    xgboost => c -> XGBoostRegressor(; seed = 1, base_score = 0.0, c...),\n    # LightGBM bags only when bagging_freq is above 0.\n    lightgbm => c -> LightGBM.MLJInterface.LGBMRegressor(; seed = 1, verbosity = -1,\n                         bagging_freq = c.bagging_fraction < 1 ? 1 : 0, c...),\n    knn => c -> KNNRegressor(; c...),\n]\n\nEvery XGBoost candidate gets base_score = 0.0, as the default model did on the previous page, where The models gives the reason: XGBoost starts from base_score, 0.5 by default, while z is centred near 0. The offset hurts most where the trees cannot remove it, with a small learning rate and few rounds. The code below scores two such candidates on validation with each base_score.\n\nfor base_score in (0.5, 0.0), num_round in (100, 200)\n    model = XGBoostRegressor(; seed = 1, eta = 0.02, num_round, base_score)\n    @printf(\"base_score = %.1f, %d rounds: %.2f\\n\", base_score, num_round,\n            validate(model)[1])\nend\n\nbase_score = 0.5, 100 rounds: 32.25\nbase_score = 0.5, 200 rounds: 15.46\nbase_score = 0.0, 100 rounds: 14.15\nbase_score = 0.0, 200 rounds: 14.06\n\nAt learning rate 0.02, 100 rounds with the default base_score score 32.25 sMAPE on validation against 14.15 with base_score = 0.0, and 200 rounds 15.46 against 14.06. Left at 0.5, the search would judge such candidates mostly by the part of the offset they had not yet removed.","title":"The search spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Tuning-on-the-validation-window","page":"Tuning the models","text":"Each of the 180 configurations is fitted on the validation panel and scored on the validation target. Each learner keeps the configuration with the lowest validation sMAPE; on a tie, the first. tried keeps every learner's candidates and validation scores for the optional diagnostic at the end of the page. This step takes most of the runtime without that diagnostic, about 13 of the 14.7 minutes on 20 threads (see the end of the page). For 12 of the 14 lasso fits, those with lambda le 10^-25, MLJLinearModels warns that proximal gradient descent did not converge in 1000 iterations, and it warns again when the chosen lasso is fitted for the holdout below. The outputs leave those warnings out, and the fits are scored as they are.\n\nchosen, tried = [], []\nprintln(\" \"^23, \"sMAPE % on validation   MASE on validation\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %10s %10s %10s %10s %7s\\n\", \"model\", \"default\", \"best\",\n        \"default\", \"best\", \"config\")\nfor (model, (configs, make)) in zip(models, searches)\n    candidates = [model; make.(configs[2:end])]\n    results = validate.(candidates)\n    i = argmin(first.(results))\n    push!(chosen, candidates[i])\n    push!(tried, (candidates, results))\n    @printf(\"%-22s %10.3f %10.3f %10.4f %10.4f %7d\\n\", nameof(typeof(model)),\n            results[1][1], results[i][1], results[1][2], results[i][2], i)\nend\n\n                       sMAPE % on validation   MASE on validation\nmodel                     default       best    default       best  config\nEvoTreeRegressor           14.169     14.092     0.8985     0.8852      17\nRandomForestRegressor      14.416     14.073     0.9279     0.8873       7\nLinearRegressor            14.513     14.512     0.9310     0.9307      23\nXGBoostRegressor           14.455     14.112     0.9197     0.8884       2\nLGBMRegressor              14.177     14.125     0.9034     0.8880      22\nKNNRegressor               14.738     14.079     0.9646     0.8872      26\n\nThe chosen configurations are evotrees[17], forest[7], linear[23], xgboost[2], lightgbm[22] and knn[26]:\n\nEvoTreeRegressor: nrounds = 800, eta = 0.02, max_depth = 5, L2 = 1.0, lambda = 1.0, min_weight = 1.0, rowsample = 0.5, colsample = 0.8.\nRandomForestRegressor: n_trees = 100, max_depth = 8, min_samples_leaf = 20, n_subfeatures = -1, sampling_fraction = 1.0.\nLinearRegressor: a LassoRegressor with lambda = 1.0e-5, level with least squares on validation (14.512 against 14.513).\nXGBoostRegressor: num_round = 800, eta = 0.02, max_depth = 8, subsample = 0.8, colsample_bytree = 1.0, min_child_weight = 50.0.\nLGBMRegressor: num_iterations = 800, learning_rate = 0.02, num_leaves = 15, min_data_in_leaf = 50, feature_fraction = 1.0, bagging_fraction = 0.6, so it bags.\nKNNRegressor: K = 75 with weights = Dudani(); the default is K = 5 with uniform weights.","title":"Tuning on the validation window"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Scoring-on-the-holdout","page":"Tuning the models","text":"Only now is the holdout used. evaluate, from the previous page, fits each learner's chosen configuration once on the full training histories, with the transform fitted on them, and scores it on the held-out 18 months. The default column repeats the previous page's results, to more decimals.\n\nprintln(\" \"^23, \"sMAPE % on the holdout   MASE on the holdout\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %10s %10s %10s %10s\\n\", \"model\", \"default\", \"tuned\", \"default\", \"tuned\")\nfor (model, tuned) in zip(models, chosen)\n    d, t = evaluate(model), evaluate(tuned)\n    @printf(\"%-22s %10.3f %10.3f %10.4f %10.4f\\n\", nameof(typeof(model)),\n            d[1], t[1], d[2], t[2])\nend\n\n                       sMAPE % on the holdout   MASE on the holdout\nmodel                     default      tuned    default      tuned\nEvoTreeRegressor           13.674     13.646     0.8539     0.8563\nRandomForestRegressor      13.663     13.707     0.8553     0.8673\nLinearRegressor            14.583     14.587     0.9909     0.9914\nXGBoostRegressor           13.776     13.647     0.8575     0.8538\nLGBMRegressor              13.706     13.729     0.8562     0.8626\nKNNRegressor               14.317     13.718     0.8978     0.8442","title":"Scoring on the holdout"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#What-tuning-changed","page":"Tuning the models","text":"In this run, tuning moved holdout sMAPE little for four learners: by -0.03 for EvoTreeRegressor, +0.04 for RandomForestRegressor, +0.02 for LGBMRegressor and +0.00 for the linear learner, and the MASE of all four got slightly worse. It helped the other two. KNNRegressor gained 0.60 sMAPE and its MASE fell from 0.898 to 0.844: its default of 5 neighbours is too small here, and the search picked 75. XGBoostRegressor gained 0.13 and its MASE fell from 0.858 to 0.854, with 800 rounds at learning rate 0.02 in place of the default 100 at 0.3. After tuning, five of the six learners score 13.65 to 13.73, while the linear learner stays near 14.58.\n\nOne 18-month validation window is a noisy guide. On validation every learner can only improve, since its default is one of the candidates, but the gain need not carry over to the holdout. The random forest and XGBoost both gained 0.34 sMAPE on validation; on the holdout XGBoost kept 0.13 of it and the random forest lost 0.04. EvoTrees and LightGBM gained 0.08 and 0.05 on validation, and changed their holdout sMAPE by -0.03 and +0.02. KNN's gain of 0.66 on validation carried over almost whole, as 0.60. The two tables are shown in full so that you can compare them yourself, and How much tuning can gain here looks closer.\n\nWhich configurations are drawn matters as well. On Julia 1.10 the same code draws other configurations for the four tree ensembles (see the end of the page). There the tuned holdout sMAPE was 13.645 for EvoTrees, 13.573 for the random forest, 13.664 for XGBoost and 13.587 for LightGBM, against 13.646, 13.707, 13.647 and 13.729 above: the random forest and LightGBM gained about 0.1 instead of losing a little, and XGBoost gained 0.11 instead of 0.13. For the tree ensembles, then, the change from tuning, at most 0.13 here, is of the same order as the change from drawing a different 29 configurations, up to 0.14. The linear and nearest-neighbour searches try their whole space and chose the same configurations on both versions.\n\nThe search loop is written out so that the page can show both sMAPE and MASE for every candidate. tune runs the same search for one metric: given vpanel, windows = 1 and horizon = 18, it scores each candidate on each series' own 18 months before the holdout, and with windows above 1 by its mean over several such windows. Pass the candidate models in grid with RandomSearch or GridSearch, or write a TuningStrategy for a search of your own.\n\nThe choice is made on sMAPE, the measure the M3 competition reported, so the note \"Why sMAPE\" under Scoring on the previous page applies here too; MASE is shown alongside for that reason.","title":"What tuning changed"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#How-much-tuning-can-gain-here","page":"Tuning the models","text":"The tables above leave two questions open for the four tree ensembles, whose tuning moved holdout sMAPE by 0.13 at most: were there better configurations among the 30 to find, and does the validation window rank the configurations as the holdout does? The optional code below answers both by scoring every configuration on the holdout. It reports the holdout sMAPE of the default, of the configuration picked on validation (tuned, as in the table above) and of the configuration that is best on the holdout (best).\n\nIt then measures how well a validation score ranks the configurations: the Spearman correlation between that score and the holdout sMAPE across the 30 configurations, 1 when the two put them in the same order and near 0 when the orders are unrelated. It is computed with cor from Statistics on ranks, and tied scores share their average rank. Four validation scores are compared: sMAPE on the validation window above (window 1); sMAPE on a second window, the 18 months before window 1 (window 2); the sMAPE of both windows pooled, with every series-window pair weighted equally (both); and MASE on window 1 (w1 MASE). Window 2 is scored as window 1 is, by evaluate on the panel with the last 36 months cut off each series, and only for the series with at least 30 months of history before it, the length of the shortest history in window 1.\n\nwarning: Optional, and for diagnosis only\nThis step is not part of the tuning: it peeks at the holdout, which a real forecast cannot do, and nothing on this page is chosen from it. It refits 240 models, 120 on the holdout and 120 on window 2, which took 22 minutes here, 17 of them for the random forest.\n\nw2 = [(id = s.id, start = s.start, y = s.y[1:(end - h)]) for s in vdated\n      if length(s.y) - 2h >= 30]\nw2panel = long(w2)\nprintln(length(w2), \" of \", length(vdated), \" series have window 2\")\n\nvalidate2(model) = evaluate(model, w2panel)[1]\n\n# Average ranks for ties, as standard Spearman uses.\nranks(x) = [count(<(v), x) + (count(==(v), x) + 1) / 2 for v in x]\nspearman(a, b) = cor(ranks(a), ranks(b))\nn1, n2 = length(vdated), length(w2)\n\nprintln(\" \"^23, \"sMAPE % on the holdout           Spearman with holdout sMAPE\")\n@printf(\"%-22s %8s %8s %8s %9s %9s %9s %9s\\n\", \"model\", \"default\", \"tuned\", \"best\",\n        \"window 1\", \"window 2\", \"both\", \"w1 MASE\")\nfor i in (1, 2, 4, 5)\n    candidates, results = tried[i]\n    holdout = [evaluate(c)[1] for c in candidates]\n    v1, v2 = first.(results), validate2.(candidates)\n    # Weight by series so every series-window pair counts once.\n    both = (n1 .* v1 .+ n2 .* v2) ./ (n1 + n2)\n    rho = [spearman(v, holdout) for v in (v1, v2, both, last.(results))]\n    @printf(\"%-22s %8.3f %8.3f %8.3f %9.2f %9.2f %9.2f %9.2f\\n\", nameof(typeof(models[i])),\n            holdout[1], holdout[argmin(v1)], minimum(holdout), rho...)\nend\n\n1081 of 1399 series have window 2\n                       sMAPE % on the holdout           Spearman with holdout sMAPE\nmodel                   default    tuned     best  window 1  window 2      both   w1 MASE\nEvoTreeRegressor         13.674   13.646   13.572      0.70      0.32      0.62      0.49\nRandomForestRegressor    13.663   13.707   13.488     -0.20      0.39     -0.14     -0.34\nXGBoostRegressor         13.776   13.647   13.565      0.55      0.46      0.53      0.30\nLGBMRegressor            13.706   13.729   13.531      0.34      0.33      0.35      0.12\n\nEven with hindsight the gain on offer is small: the configuration that is best on the holdout beats the default by 0.10 sMAPE for EvoTrees, 0.18 for the random forest, 0.21 for XGBoost and 0.18 for LightGBM. The configurations picked on validation took 0.03 of that for EvoTrees and 0.13 for XGBoost, and lost a little for the other two.\n\nHere validation ranks the configurations unreliably. On window 1 the Spearman correlation with the holdout is 0.70 for EvoTrees, 0.55 for XGBoost, 0.34 for LightGBM and -0.20 for the random forest, whose validation order is unrelated to its holdout order. A second window does not fix this. Window 2 alone gives 0.32 to 0.46: better than window 1 for the random forest, worse for EvoTrees and XGBoost, level for LightGBM. Pooling both windows gives 0.62, -0.14, 0.53 and 0.35 for EvoTrees, the random forest, XGBoost and LightGBM, close to window 1 alone. Ranking by MASE on window 1 agrees less with the holdout than ranking by sMAPE, for all four learners.\n\nOn Julia 1.10, whose searches draw other configurations (see the end of the page), the same code gives other numbers. For EvoTrees, the random forest, XGBoost and LightGBM, the best configuration in hindsight beats the default by 0.11, 0.16, 0.20 and 0.12, again small. The Spearman correlations are 0.56, 0.09, 0.74 and 0.72 on window 1, 0.24, 0.42, 0.71 and 0.57 on window 2, and 0.43, 0.19, 0.74 and 0.66 pooled, and ranking by MASE again agrees less for all but the random forest. There validation ranks the XGBoost and LightGBM configurations well, and LightGBM's pick is its best, so how well one window ranks the configurations depends on which ones are drawn as well as on the learner.\n\nThe gains at stake, about 0.1 to 0.2 sMAPE, are no larger than how much a configuration's lead over the default changes from one 18-month window to the next: the random forest's pick led by 0.34 on validation and trailed by 0.04 on the holdout. On this panel, with these search spaces, the defaults of the tree ensembles are within about 0.2 sMAPE of the best of their 30 configurations, found in hindsight, on both versions, and the pick on window 1 was that configuration in one of the eight searches, LightGBM's on Julia 1.10. These are findings for this panel and these spaces, not general ones.\n\nTested with Julia 1.12.5 and 1.10.12, each started with --threads=auto (20 threads), running the previous page's code and this page's as one script. The outputs above are from Julia 1.12.5, where the script took 36 minutes, 14.7 of them before the optional diagnostic, and peaked at about 5.5 GiB of memory. On 1.10.12 it took 29 minutes, 11.7 before the diagnostic, and peaked at about 17.4 GiB. Julia 1.11 changed how MersenneTwister turns an integer seed into its state, so on Julia 1.10 MersenneTwister(2026) gives other random numbers and the four random searches draw other configurations. The defaults and the linear and nearest-neighbour searches score the same on both versions. Package versions are those listed on the previous page.","title":"How much tuning can gain here"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Leakage-and-conventions","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"This page states what the package guarantees against leakage, the conventions you need to know to read a forecast or a backtest score correctly, how to choose between Recursive and Direct, and recipes for custom features. The code blocks are plain Julia: they show the pattern and are not run when the documentation is built.","title":"Leakage and conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#What-the-package-guarantees","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"Every target-history feature (TargetFeature) uses only the target values strictly before the current row's target, so rolling windows and differences require lag ≥ 1 and Lag requires k ≥ 1. A test compares every built-in feature's training columns with its forecast-time values and rewrites the target from a row onward to check that earlier rows do not change. backtest fits each fold, including its target transform, on the rows up to the fold's origin only.\n\nOne case is yours to keep: a CustomFeature function must read only the history it is given. A function that reads a captured series or parent(h) bypasses the guarantee. When the forecaster has a target transform, the history is the transformed target.","title":"What the package guarantees"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Conventions","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"Lag under Direct. Under Direct, Lag(k) counts back from the forecast origin, so at horizon h it is y[t0-k+1] (t0 is the index of the last observed value), not y[target-k]; Lag(m) is in phase with the target only when h = 1 (mod m); use Lag.(1:m), which holds an in-phase lag at every h, or Recursive.\n\nExogenous and backtest. A backtest uses the realised values of the covariates, so it scores ex-post forecasts (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos 2021, §7.6). For covariates not known at the origin (a competitor's price, the weather) this typically understates the error of the real, ex-ante forecast, which also carries the error of forecasting the covariate. For covariates known in advance (calendar effects, planned promotions, prices you set) ex-ante and ex-post agree. To measure ex-ante error, supply in the held-out rows the covariate values that were available at the origin (a forecast, or a lagged column). The recipes below build lagged columns.\n\nPanel backtest. A fold does not score a series that has no rows up to the fold's origin, too few of them to train on (it is skipped with a warning), or no actuals after it, so the set of scored series can change from fold to fold. With windows, each series is cut at its own end, as in the M competitions. If the series are not aligned in calendar time, the training set of a fold can contain values from after another series' cutoff, a known pitfall of global models (Hewamalage, Ackermann & Bergmeir 2023). With initial, all series are cut at the same timestamp, which avoids it.\n\nGaps and missing values. The package refuses a time column with gaps, duplicates or missing timestamps, and a target with missing or non-finite values. Fill them causally (forward fill, or the mean of past values) before fitting, because a fill that uses later values, such as interpolation, leaks. Exogenous columns may hold NaN, which reaches the model as a missing marker, but not missing or ±Inf.\n\nAggregation of metrics. Unscaled metrics pool the fold's rows; scaled metrics are per series, weighted by scored points; summarize gives the unweighted mean over folds. Scaled metrics are those whose metric_inputs is Scaled(), such as mase and rmsse, or their callable forms Mase and Rmsse. Scaled metrics follow Hyndman & Koehler (2006): each series' errors are divided by that series' own training-window naive error, then averaged over series (here weighted by scored points, our choice). One series with a constant training window has a zero scale, which makes that fold's score, and so its mean over folds in summarize, Inf. Unscaled metrics are pooled across series, so on a panel they are dominated by the large-scale series; use a scaled metric to compare series of different scale. On intermittent series (many zeros), MAE-based measures, including MASE, can favour forecasts close to zero; the M5 competition used a squared-error scaled measure (RMSSE) for this reason (Makridakis, Spiliotis & Assimakopoulos 2022); use rmsse for such series. Both take the scale from the whole training window; for the M5 rule, which starts it at the first non-zero value, see the recipe below.\n\nTuning base models with MLJ. If you tune the base model with MLJ's TunedModel, prefer TimeSeriesCV to CV(). Plain K-fold CV trains on rows that come after some of its test rows. For a stationary series, pure lag features and a model whose one-step residuals are not autocorrelated, this does not bias the estimate (Bergmeir, Hyndman & Koo 2018); it does for a trending or otherwise non-stationary series (Cerqueira, Torgo & Mozetič 2020) or when the model underfits and its residuals are autocorrelated. The result does not cover covariates or multi-step targets. TimeSeriesCV never trains on rows after its test rows on a single series, so it avoids these problems there. On a panel the pooled frame is ordered by series, not by time, so score on a validation window or use tune instead. Tuning the models scores on a validation window that comes after the training window.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Choosing-a-strategy","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"Recursive fits one model on one-step-ahead rows, where the lag features are the true past values. At forecast time it runs that model on its own earlier predictions, and any horizon can be forecast.\n\nDirect fits one model per step 1..max_horizon. Model i predicts the target i steps ahead from the history at the forecast origin, so no prediction is fed back. It needs max_horizon models, cannot forecast past max_horizon, and every step sees the same target history, which is why the Lag convention above matters. On a panel, a series with fewer than max_horizon training rows trains only the step models it has rows for, and fit warns.\n\nNeither strategy is better in general. Recursive estimates one model efficiently but is biased when the one-step model is misspecified, and the errors it feeds back accumulate. Direct is more robust to such misspecification and to non-stationarity such as unit roots, and feeds nothing back, but estimates each horizon separately, which costs variance, and its forecasts at neighbouring steps need not be coherent (Marcellino, Stock & Watson 2006; Chevillon 2007; Ben Taieb et al. 2012). Empirically, iterated (Recursive) forecasts are often at least as accurate on stationary data (Marcellino, Stock & Watson 2006). Compare both with backtest, or let tune search over strategy.","title":"Choosing a strategy"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Recipes","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"Each recipe assumes df is a daily series with the time column :ds and the target :y, panel the same as a long table with an id column :unique_id, and model an MLJ regressor. The recipes were run on small synthetic series before they were written down.","title":"Recipes"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Rolling-median,-exponentially-weighted-mean,-zeros","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"CustomFeature turns a function of the history into a feature. The history h is a vector in forecasting and a view in training: read it, do not keep it. In training the function is called once per row, so keep it cheap; the window below bounds the cost of the exponentially weighted mean.\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast, Dates, Statistics\n\nfunction ewm(h, α; window = 30)\n    s = h[max(1, end - window + 1)]\n    for v in @view h[max(1, end - window + 2):end]\n        s = α * v + (1 - α) * s\n    end\n    return s\nend\n\nfeatures = FeatureSet(\n    Lag(1),\n    CustomFeature(:roll_median_7, h -> median(@view h[end-6:end]), 7),\n    CustomFeature(:ewm_03, h -> ewm(h, 0.3), 1),\n    # Share of zeros in the last 28 values (fewer at the start).\n    CustomFeature(:zero_share_28,\n                  h -> count(iszero, @view h[max(1, end - 27):end]) / min(28, length(h)),\n                  1),\n    # Steps since the last non-zero value; 0 when the last value is non-zero.\n    CustomFeature(:since_nonzero,\n                  h -> (i = findlast(!iszero, h);\n                        i === nothing ? length(h) : length(h) - i),\n                  1),\n)\nfc = Forecaster(model; features, freq = Day(1))\n\nminhistory is the number of history values the function needs: 7 for the median, which reads the last 7. All four work under Recursive and Direct, and in backtest.","title":"Rolling median, exponentially weighted mean, zeros"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#A-holiday-feature","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"A feature that depends on the date only subtypes TimeFeature. It needs outputnames and featurevalues!; minhistory is 0 for every time feature, and the default materialize! fills the training column by calling featurevalues! on every row.\n\nusing Dates\nimport MachineLearningForecast: outputnames, featurevalues!\n\nstruct Holiday <: TimeFeature\n    days::Set{Date}\nend\noutputnames(::Holiday) = [:is_holiday]\nfeaturevalues!(out, f::Holiday, ctx) = (out[1] = Float64(Date(ctx.t) in f.days); nothing)\n\nholidays = Holiday(Set([Date(2023, 1, 1), Date(2023, 5, 1), Date(2023, 12, 25)]))\nfc = Forecaster(model; features = FeatureSet(Lag(1), holidays), freq = Day(1))\n\nctx.t is the timestamp of the row's target, so the feature needs no future table. See featurevalues! and anchor for the contract.","title":"A holiday feature"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#A-parent-aggregate-as-a-covariate","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"The total over all series (the parent in a hierarchy) is not known for the forecast period, but its value H steps ago is, as long as H is at least the forecast horizon. Shift it by H, then pass it through Exogenous. The recipe assumes that every series ends on the same date and that the dates are contiguous. The column holds only values from before the forecast origin in fit, in forecast and in backtest, so the backtest measures the ex-ante error.\n\nH = 7                                   # at least the horizon\ndates = sort(unique(panel.ds))\ntotal = [sum(panel.y[panel.ds .== d]) for d in dates]\n# The parent's value H steps before each date.\nlagged = Dict(d + H * Day(1) => v for (d, v) in zip(dates, total))\n\n# The first H dates have no lagged value: leave them out of the training data.\nkeep = [haskey(lagged, d) for d in panel.ds]\ntrain = (unique_id = panel.unique_id[keep], ds = panel.ds[keep], y = panel.y[keep],\n         parent_lag = [lagged[d] for d in panel.ds[keep]])\n\npanel_fc = Forecaster(model; features = FeatureSet(Lag(1), Exogenous(:parent_lag)),\n                      freq = Day(1), id = :unique_id)\npanel_fitted = fit(panel_fc, train)\n\nstores = unique(panel.unique_id)\nfuture_dates = collect(dates[end] + Day(1):Day(1):dates[end] + Day(H))\nfuture_data = (unique_id = repeat(stores, inner = H), ds = repeat(future_dates, length(stores)),\n               parent_lag = [lagged[d] for _ in stores for d in future_dates])\nforecast(panel_fitted, H; future = future_data)\nbacktest(panel_fc, train; horizon = H, windows = 2)\n\nWith a horizon above H, the lagged column would hold values from after the origin, and the backtest would leak them.","title":"A parent aggregate as a covariate"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#A-covariate-shifted-by-at-least-the-horizon","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"The same rule works for any covariate: shift it by k ≥ horizon and the column is known at the origin. For a single series with a price vector:\n\nk = 14                                  # at least the horizon\nh = 7\nsingle = (ds = df.ds[(k + 1):end], y = df.y[(k + 1):end],\n          price_lag14 = price[1:(end - k)])\nfc = Forecaster(model; features = FeatureSet(Lag(1), Exogenous(:price_lag14)),\n                freq = Day(1))\nfitted = fit(fc, single)\n\nn = length(df.y)\nfuture = (ds = df.ds[end] .+ Day.(1:h), price_lag14 = price[(n - k + 1):(n - k + h)])\nforecast(fitted, h; future)","title":"A covariate shifted by at least the horizon"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Scaling-from-the-first-non-zero-value-(the-M5-rule)","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"mase and rmsse take the scale from the whole training window (Hyndman & Koehler 2006; Hyndman & Athanasopoulos 2021, §5.8). The M5 competition computed it only from the first non-zero value on, because products there launch late and the leading zeros mean \"not yet launched\" (Makridakis, Spiliotis & Assimakopoulos 2022; M Open Forecasting Center 2020). To score that way, trim y_train to start at its first non-zero value, that value included, and delegate:\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast\nimport MachineLearningForecast: metric_inputs, Scaled\n\nfunction from_first_nonzero(y_train)\n    i = findfirst(!iszero, y_train)\n    return i === nothing ? y_train : y_train[i:end]\nend\n\nrmsse_m5(y, ŷ; y_train, m = 1) = rmsse(y, ŷ; y_train = from_first_nonzero(y_train), m)\nmase_m5(y, ŷ; y_train, m = 1) = mase(y, ŷ; y_train = from_first_nonzero(y_train), m)\nmetric_inputs(::typeof(rmsse_m5)) = Scaled()\nmetric_inputs(::typeof(mase_m5)) = Scaled()\n\nrmsse_m5([2.0, 3.0], [2.0, 2.0]; y_train = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 4.0, 6.0, 5.0, 7.0])\n\nPass rmsse_m5 or mase_m5 to backtest or tune like any metric. A metric with a fixed m, such as Mase(12), is a plain const (see mase12 in Models).\n\nTrim only when leading zeros mean \"not yet launched\". Where they are genuine zero demand, they belong to the series and trimming discards them. An all-zero window has nothing to trim, so it is passed on unchanged: the scale is zero, and the metric warns and returns Inf, as rmsse and mase do for any constant window; the same happens when the values from the first non-zero on are constant. A window with m or fewer values from its first non-zero value on is too short for the scale, so rmsse and mase throw their usual ArgumentError (length(y_train) > m), and a backtest over such a series fails.","title":"Scaling from the first non-zero value (the M5 rule)"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Scores-per-horizon-step-and-per-series","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"BacktestResult.forecasts has one row per fold, step and series with :step, :y and :y_hat (and the id column for a panel), so any score is a few lines. This one scores panel_fc, train and H from the parent aggregate recipe:\n\nusing Statistics\nr = backtest(panel_fc, train; horizon = H, windows = 3)\nf = r.forecasts\nerr = abs.(f.y .- f.y_hat)\nby_step = [mean(err[f.step .== s]) for s in sort(unique(f.step))]\nby_series = Dict(id => mean(err[f.unique_id .== id]) for id in unique(f.unique_id))","title":"Scores per horizon step and per series"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#A-naive-forecaster-as-a-baseline","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"A model that returns y_lag_1 is a naive forecaster: under Recursive every step repeats the last observed value. Backtesting it gives the score to beat.\n\nNaiveLast is an MLJ model, so it needs MLJModelInterface (for MMI) and Tables.jl in your own environment.\n\nusing Tables\nimport MLJModelInterface as MMI\n\nmutable struct NaiveLast <: MMI.Deterministic end\nMMI.fit(::NaiveLast, verbosity, X, y) = (nothing, nothing, NamedTuple())\nMMI.predict(::NaiveLast, fitresult, Xnew) =\n    collect(Float64, Tables.getcolumn(Xnew, :y_lag_1))\nMMI.input_scitype(::Type{NaiveLast}) = MMI.Table(MMI.Continuous)\nMMI.target_scitype(::Type{NaiveLast}) = AbstractVector{MMI.Continuous}\n\nnaive = Forecaster(NaiveLast(); features = FeatureSet(Lag(1)), freq = Day(1))\nsummarize(backtest(naive, df; horizon = 7, windows = 3, metrics = (mae, smape)))","title":"A naive forecaster as a baseline"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#A-seasonal-naive-forecaster-as-a-baseline","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"Naive and seasonal naive forecasts are the standard benchmarks (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos 2021, §5.2). When the data are seasonal with period m, backtest SeasonalNaive(m): a model that returns y_lag_m, with Lag(m) as the only feature, under Recursive. For a horizon above m the fed-back predictions equal the lagged values, so each step repeats the value one or more seasons earlier, which is the seasonal naive forecast. Under Direct it does not: Lag(m) is then y[t0-m+1] at every step, as explained above.\n\nm = 7\nsnaive = Forecaster(SeasonalNaive(m); features = FeatureSet(Lag(m)), freq = Day(1))\nsummarize(backtest(snaive, df; horizon = 14, windows = 3, metrics = (mae, smape)))","title":"A seasonal naive forecaster as a baseline"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#References","page":"Leakage and conventions","text":"Ben Taieb, S., Bontempi, G., Atiya, A. F., & Sorjamaa, A. (2012). A review and comparison of strategies for multi-step ahead time series forecasting based on the NN5 forecasting competition. Expert Systems with Applications, 39(8), 7067–7083. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2012.01.039\nBergmeir, C., Hyndman, R. J., & Koo, B. (2018). A note on the validity of cross-validation for evaluating autoregressive time series prediction. Computational Statistics & Data Analysis, 120, 70–83. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.csda.2017.11.003\nCerqueira, V., Torgo, L., & Mozetič, I. (2020). Evaluating time series forecasting models: an empirical study on performance estimation methods. Machine Learning, 109(11), 1997–2028. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-020-05910-7\nChevillon, G. (2007). Direct multi-step estimation and forecasting. Journal of Economic Surveys, 21(4), 746–785. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-6419.2007.00518.x\nHewamalage, H., Ackermann, K., & Bergmeir, C. (2023). Forecast evaluation for data scientists: common pitfalls and best practices. Data Mining and Knowledge Discovery, 37(2), 788–832. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10618-022-00894-5\nHyndman, R. J., & Athanasopoulos, G. (2021). Forecasting: principles and practice (3rd ed.). OTexts. https://otexts.com/fpp3/\nHyndman, R. J., & Koehler, A. B. (2006). Another look at measures of forecast accuracy. International Journal of Forecasting, 22(4), 679–688. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijforecast.2006.03.001\nM Open Forecasting Center (2020). The M5 competition: competitors' guide. University of Nicosia. https://github.com/Mcompetitions/M5-methods/blob/master/M5-Competitors-Guide.pdf\nMakridakis, S., Spiliotis, E., & Assimakopoulos, V. (2022). M5 accuracy competition: results, findings, and conclusions. International Journal of Forecasting, 38(4), 1346–1364. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijforecast.2021.11.013\nMarcellino, M., Stock, J. H., & Watson, M. W. (2006). A comparison of direct and iterated multistep AR methods for forecasting macroeconomic time series. Journal of Econometrics, 135(1–2), 499–526. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jeconom.2005.07.020","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.jl","page":"Home","text":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\">\n  <img class=\"docs-light-only\" src=\"assets/logo-wide-tagline.svg\"\n       alt=\"MachineLearningForecast.jl logo\" width=\"520\"/>\n  <img class=\"docs-dark-only\" src=\"assets/logo-wide-tagline-dark.svg\"\n       alt=\"MachineLearningForecast.jl logo\" width=\"520\"/>\n</p>\n\nGeneric, composable time-series forecasting built on the MLJ model interface: any MLJ Deterministic regressor is a valid base model.\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast, EvoTrees, Dates\n\ndf = (ds = collect(Date(2020,1,1):Day(1):Date(2023,12,31)),\n      y  = randn(1461) .+ 10)\n\nfc = Forecaster(EvoTreeRegressor(nrounds=200, eta=0.05);\n                features = FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7), RollingMean(7),\n                                      Calendar(:dayofweek), Fourier(365.25, 3)),\n                strategy = Recursive(),\n                freq     = Day(1))\n\nfitted = fit(fc, df)\nfcast  = forecast(fitted, 28)\n\nMachineLearningForecast is Tables.jl-native: any Tables.jl-compatible source is accepted (NamedTuple of vectors, DataFrame, CSV.File, ...), and all tabular results are columntables (NamedTuples of vectors).\n\nSee the README for the full tour: feature catalogue, strategies, backtesting, and the two-level tuning story — including the leakage warning about preferring TimeSeriesCV to MLJ's CV() when tuning base models with MLJ's TunedModel.\n\nLeakage and conventions states what the package guarantees against leakage, the conventions behind forecasts and backtest scores, how to choose a strategy, and recipes for custom features. Using different models compares six MLJ learners on the M3 monthly series. Tuning the models tunes their hyperparameters on a validation window.","title":"MachineLearningForecast.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#API-reference","page":"Home","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"#Forecasting","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Forecasting"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Target-transforms","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Target transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"#Backtesting-and-metrics","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Backtesting and metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"#Probabilistic-forecasts","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Probabilistic forecasts"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tuning","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Tuning"},{"category":"module","location":"#MachineLearningForecast","page":"Home","text":"MachineLearningForecast\n\nA generic, composable time-series forecasting package built on the MLJ model interface: any MLJ Deterministic regressor is a valid base model, and a Probabilistic one forecasts its predicted mean.\n\nDeclarative feature engineering: FeatureSet, Lag, RollingMean, RollingStd, RollingMin, RollingMax, Diff, Calendar, Fourier, Exogenous, CustomFeature.\nPluggable strategies: Recursive, Direct.\nTarget transforms, fitted per series and undone on the forecasts: NoTransform, MeanScale, LastScale, Deseasonalize, LogRatio, Difference, TransformChain; your own subtype TargetTransform implements fit_transform, untransform and, if it shortens the series, dropped_rows (public, not exported: import MachineLearningForecast: fit_transform, untransform, dropped_rows).\nPanel (multi-series) forecasting: set id on Forecaster to fit one global model across many series (nseries, SeriesState).\nfit / forecast as a functional pair; backtest for expanding-window cross-validation, summarize for its scores; tune for pipeline tuning with an extensible ask!/tell! TuningStrategy interface.\nMetrics: mae, rmse, mape, smape, mase, rmsse, and their callable forms Mase and Rmsse; the probabilistic scores crps, fair_crps, pinball_loss, Coverage and IntervalWidth; your own metric implements metric_inputs and, to be named, metric_name (public, not exported: import MachineLearningForecast: metric_inputs, metric_name).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Forecaster","page":"Home","text":"Forecaster(model; features, strategy=Recursive(), freq, target=:y, time=:ds, id=nothing,\n           transform=NoTransform(), intervals=nothing)\n\nImmutable forecasting specification.\n\nmodel: any MLJ Deterministic regressor instance (EvoTrees, DecisionTree, XGBoost, linear models, ... — anything implementing the MLJ model interface), or a Probabilistic one, whose predicted mean is the forecast. Used as a prototype; fit never mutates it.\nfeatures: a FeatureSet describing the design matrix.\nstrategy: a ForecastStrategy — Recursive (default) or Direct.\nfreq: the series frequency as a positive Dates.Period, e.g. Day(1), Month(1); a sum such as Day(1) + Hour(1) is not accepted.\ntarget: name of the target column (default :y).\ntime: name of the time column (default :ds), of a type supporting + with freq (Date, DateTime).\nid: name of the series-id column for panel (multi-series) data, or nothing (the default) for a single series. When set, data is a long-format panel: one row per (series, timestamp). Features are materialised within each series — a lag never reaches across a series boundary — and the rows of every series are then pooled to fit one global model, so short or noisy series borrow strength from the rest. Series may be ragged (different start dates, end dates and lengths); each forecasts forward from its own last timestamp. forecast then returns an id column alongside the timestamps.\ntransform: a TargetTransform applied to each series' target before features are built, e.g. LastScale or TransformChain(LastScale(12), Deseasonalize(12), LogRatio(12)). Its state is fitted per series on the training history only, and forecasts are mapped back to the original scale. The default NoTransform leaves the target as given.\nintervals: nothing (the default), or a PoolIntervals interval method (public, not exported: using MachineLearningForecast: PoolIntervals): Conformal Seasonal Pools around the seasonal naive forecast. It is accepted only with a SeasonalNaive model of the same period, exactly FeatureSet(Lag(period)), Recursive() and no transform; ConformalSeasonalPool builds that setup.\n\nFit with fit, which returns a FittedForecaster; the spec itself is never mutated.\n\nExample\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast, Dates\nfc = Forecaster(model;\n                features=FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7), Calendar(:dayofweek)),\n                strategy=Recursive(), freq=Day(1), target=:y, time=:ds)\n\n# a panel of many series, fitted as one global model\npanel = Forecaster(model;\n                   features=FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7)),\n                   strategy=Recursive(), freq=Day(1),\n                   target=:y, time=:ds, id=:unique_id)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Forecaster"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.FittedForecaster","page":"Home","text":"FittedForecaster\n\nThe result of fit: the immutable spec plus everything needed to forecast — the trained models (one for Recursive, max_horizon for Direct), the training target history (needed to compute lag features at forecast time), the first/last training timestamps, and the stable feature column order. Use with forecast.\n\nfitted.models holds the trained models, each with the trained copy of the spec's model as .model and its MLJ fitresult as .fitresult. fitted.series holds one SeriesState per series. For a single series, fitted.y_history, fitted.t_last, fitted.t_start and fitted.n_train read its one state directly; on a panel they throw an ArgumentError pointing at fitted.series.\n\nExample\n\nfitted = fit(fc, df)\nfitted.models        # the trained model(s)\nfitted.series        # one SeriesState per series\nfitted.t_last        # single series only: the last training timestamp\nforecast(fitted, 28)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.FittedForecaster"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TrainedModel","page":"Home","text":"TrainedModel{M,R}\n\nOne model trained by fit: model is the copy of the spec's model that was trained, and fitresult is what its MLJModelInterface.fit returned. Pass both to MLJ's interface functions to inspect it, e.g. MLJModelInterface.fitted_params(t.model, t.fitresult).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TrainedModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.SeriesState","page":"Home","text":"SeriesState\n\nEverything forecast needs about one series: its id (nothing for a single-series forecaster), the training target history that lag and rolling features are computed from, the first and last training timestamps, and the number of training rows. z_history is the history after the spec's TargetTransform, which lag and rolling features and the step index use, and transform_state is what untransform needs; without a transform, z_history is y_history. step0 is the context's step index (ctx.step, see FeatureContext) of the first z_history value: 0 for a single series, and in a panel the steps from the earliest usable series' first (transformed) timestamp.\n\nRead them off a fitted panel forecaster via f.series; for a single-series forecaster the same values are reachable directly as f.y_history, f.t_last, f.t_start and f.n_train.\n\nExample\n\nf = fit(panel_spec, panel)\nfor st in f.series\n    println(st.id, \": \", st.n_train, \" rows ending \", st.t_last)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.SeriesState"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.nseries","page":"Home","text":"nseries(f::FittedForecaster) -> Int\n\nThe number of series the forecaster was fitted on: 1 for a single series, or the number of usable series in the panel. Series too short for the feature set are skipped at fit time, so this can be smaller than the number of ids in the input.\n\nExample\n\nusing Dates\nf = fit(Forecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), freq=Day(1), id=:unique_id),\n        panel)\nnseries(f)                       # e.g. 3\n[s.id for s in f.series]         # the ids, in first-appearance order\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.nseries"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.fit","page":"Home","text":"fit(fc::Forecaster, data) -> FittedForecaster\n\nFit the forecaster on data, any Tables.jl-compatible table containing the spec's time and target columns (plus any exogenous columns, and the id column for a panel). For a single series the time column must be sorted, duplicate-free, and gap-free with respect to fc.freq. For a panel, rows may come in any order: fit sorts each series by time, and each series must be duplicate-free and gap-free.\n\nfit is a function owned by MachineLearningForecast (not an extension of MLJBase.fit or StatsAPI.fit); qualify as MachineLearningForecast.fit when another fit is in scope.\n\nExample\n\nfitted = fit(fc, df)\nfcast  = forecast(fitted, 28)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.fit"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.forecast","page":"Home","text":"forecast(f::FittedForecaster, h::Integer; future=nothing) -> NamedTuple\n\nForecast h steps past the end of the training data. Returns a columntable (NamedTuple of vectors) with the spec's time column (the future timestamps t_last + freq, ..., t_last + h*freq) and :y_hat (the point forecasts). Being a columntable, the result is Tables.jl-compatible — pass it to DataFrame, CSV.write, etc. as-is.\n\nIf the feature set contains any Exogenous feature, future is required: a table with the time column and every exogenous column, covering all h future timestamps (extra rows are ignored). Without exogenous features, future must be omitted (it is ignored with a warning otherwise).\n\nFor a panel forecaster (one with id set), the result also has the id column, before the time column: h rows per series, each series forecast from its own last training timestamp. future must then carry the id column too and cover every series at each of its h future timestamps.\n\nExample\n\nfcast = forecast(fitted, 28)\nfcast = forecast(fitted, 28; future=future_exog)   # with Exogenous features\nfcast.y_hat                                        # the point forecasts\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.forecast"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Recursive","page":"Home","text":"Recursive()\n\nOne model; forecasts are fed back as pseudo-history to compute lag features. The model is trained once on the one-step-ahead frame and iterated over the horizon, so any horizon can be forecast. Neither strategy is better in general: Recursive estimates one model efficiently but is biased when the one-step model is misspecified, and the errors it feeds back accumulate (references under Direct).\n\nExample\n\nusing Dates\nForecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), strategy=Recursive(), freq=Day(1))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Recursive"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Direct","page":"Home","text":"Direct(max_horizon)\n\nOne model per horizon step 1..max_horizon. max_horizon fixes the number of models at fit time; forecasting beyond it errors (refit with a larger horizon or use Recursive). On a panel, a series with fewer than max_horizon training rows trains only the step models it has rows for, and fit warns.\n\nNeither strategy is better in general. Recursive estimates one model efficiently but is biased when the one-step model is misspecified, and the errors it feeds back accumulate. Direct is more robust to such misspecification and to non-stationarity such as unit roots, and feeds nothing back, but estimates each horizon separately, which costs variance, and its forecasts at neighbouring steps need not be coherent (Marcellino, Stock & Watson 2006; Chevillon 2007; Ben Taieb et al. 2012). Empirically, iterated (Recursive) forecasts are often at least as accurate on stationary data (Marcellino, Stock & Watson 2006). Compare both with backtest.\n\nAt forecast time, the direct models condition their target-history features on the history up to the forecast origin, while exogenous and time features vary per step.\n\nUnder Direct, Lag(k) counts back from the forecast origin, so at horizon h it is y[t0-k+1] (t0 is the index of the last observed value), not y[target-k]; Lag(m) is in phase with the target only when h = 1 (mod m); use Lag.(1:m), which holds an in-phase lag at every h, or Recursive.\n\nModel i predicts the target i steps ahead. At fit time its AtOrigin features (lags, rolling statistics, differences, custom) come from the feature row j, and its AtTarget features (time and exogenous) come from the target row j+i-1, which is what forecast feeds at step i; see anchor. Taking both from row j would be a train/serve skew that silently degrades every step after the first.\n\nExample\n\nusing Dates\nForecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), strategy=Direct(28), freq=Day(1))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Direct"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.ForecastStrategy","page":"Home","text":"ForecastStrategy\n\nAbstract supertype of forecasting strategies. Shipped strategies: Recursive and Direct. It is the type of the strategy field of a Forecaster, and tuning grids and dispatch can use it.\n\nExample\n\nstrategies = [Recursive(), Direct(28)]\nall(s -> s isa ForecastStrategy, strategies)   # true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.ForecastStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.FeatureSet","page":"Home","text":"FeatureSet(features::AbstractFeature...)\nFeatureSet(features::AbstractVector{<:AbstractFeature})\n\nAn ordered collection of features. Order matters: it fixes the column order of the design matrix fed to the model. Output column names must be unique across the whole set.\n\nExample\n\nFeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7), RollingMean(7), Calendar(:dayofweek), Fourier(365.25, 3))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.FeatureSet"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.outputnames","page":"Home","text":"outputnames(fs::FeatureSet) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nAll output column names produced by the set, in stable order (feature order, then each feature's own column order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.outputnames"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.minhistory","page":"Home","text":"minhistory(fs::FeatureSet) -> Int\n\nThe number of leading rows for which at least one feature is undefined; these rows are dropped from the training frame.\n\n\n\n\n\nminhistory(fc::Forecaster) -> Int\n\nThe number of rows a series must have more than for fc to train on it: the feature set's minhistory plus the rows the target transform drops. fit skips a panel series with this many rows or fewer, and a backtest initial must exceed it.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: minhistory\n\nfc = Forecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), freq=Day(1),\n                transform=TransformChain(Difference(12), LastScale(3)))\nminhistory(fc)   # 13: one history row plus the 12 the transform drops\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.minhistory"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.inputcolumns","page":"Home","text":"inputcolumns(f) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe user columns the feature f reads. forecast and backtest pick exactly these columns from future (or from the held-out rows), and training reads them from the training data too, so they must be present there: fit throws otherwise (unless the feature overrides materialize!). The default is Symbol[], Exogenous returns its columns, and an ExogenousFeature of your own must define it, otherwise an ArgumentError is thrown. Any feature may define it.\n\nExample\n\ninputcolumns(Exogenous(:promo, :price))   # [:promo, :price]\ninputcolumns(Lag(1))                      # Symbol[]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.inputcolumns"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.FeatureContext","page":"Home","text":"FeatureContext(y, t, step, exog)\n\nThe row a feature is asked about, passed to featurevalues!. It is read-only: do not keep a reference to it or to y.\n\ny: the target history strictly before the row's target (a Vector when forecasting, a view in training). It is empty at the first row when minhistory(f) == 0.\nt: the timestamp of the row's target.\nstep: the 0-based step index of the row's target. It counts freq steps from the first (transformed) training timestamp, and from the earliest usable series' first (transformed) timestamp on a panel.\nexog: a NamedTuple of the user columns at the row (it may hold more than inputcolumns(f) when forecasting, so index it by name). When inputcolumns(f) is empty it may be nothing or a NamedTuple (forecasting with another exogenous feature): do not read it then.\n\nExample\n\nusing Dates\nctx = MachineLearningForecast.FeatureContext([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], Date(2022, 1, 4), 3, nothing)\nctx.y[end]   # 3.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.FeatureContext"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.featurevalues!","page":"Home","text":"featurevalues!(out, f, ctx)\n\nWrite the values of feature f for the row that the FeatureContext ctx describes into out, a Vector{Float64} of length(outputnames(f)) entries (write every one of them); the return value is ignored. This is the one method a feature must implement for its values: training, forecast and backtest all call it, so by default (unless the feature overrides materialize!) they cannot disagree.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: outputnames, minhistory, featurevalues!\n\nstruct LastTwo <: MachineLearningForecast.TargetFeature end\noutputnames(::LastTwo) = [:last_two]\nminhistory(::LastTwo) = 2\nfeaturevalues!(out, ::LastTwo, ctx) = (out[1] = ctx.y[end] + ctx.y[end-1]; nothing)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.featurevalues!"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.materialize!","page":"Home","text":"materialize!(out, f, s::TrainingSeries)\n\nPush the training columns of feature f over the series s onto out (a vector of name => column pairs), with missing in the first minhistory(f) rows. The default calls featurevalues! for every row from minhistory(f) + 1 on, so a feature gets this for free; it leaves a row missing when one of its inputcolumns is missing there. Define a method only for speed or to validate a column before any row is read, as Calendar, Fourier and Exogenous do.\n\nExample\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast\nusing MachineLearningForecast: ColumnAccumulator, TrainingSeries, materialize!\nout = ColumnAccumulator()\nmaterialize!(out, Lag(1), TrainingSeries([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 1:3, (;), 0))\nout   # [:y_lag_1 => [missing, 1.0, 2.0]]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.materialize!"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TrainingSeries","page":"Home","text":"TrainingSeries(y, t, data, step0)\n\nWhat materialize! reads: one series' training columns. y is the (transformed) target, t the time column, data the whole columntable (for the columns named by inputcolumns) and step0 the step index of row 1, so that row i has ctx.step == step0 + i - 1.\n\nExample\n\nusing Dates\ns = MachineLearningForecast.TrainingSeries([1.0, 2.0], [Date(2022, 1, 1), Date(2022, 1, 2)],\n                                           (;), 0)\ns.step0   # 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TrainingSeries"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Anchor","page":"Home","text":"Anchor\n\nAbstract supertype of AtOrigin and AtTarget: which row a feature's columns describe. See anchor.\n\nExample\n\nanchor(Lag(1)) isa Anchor   # true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Anchor"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.AtOrigin","page":"Home","text":"AtOrigin()\n\nThe anchor of a feature whose columns describe the feature row, that is the target history up to the forecast origin (lags, rolling statistics).\n\nExample\n\nanchor(Lag(1))   # AtOrigin()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.AtOrigin"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.AtTarget","page":"Home","text":"AtTarget()\n\nThe anchor of a feature whose columns describe the row being predicted (calendar, Fourier and exogenous columns).\n\nExample\n\nanchor(Calendar(:month))   # AtTarget()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.AtTarget"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.anchor","page":"Home","text":"anchor(f) -> Anchor\n\nWhich row the columns of feature f describe, AtOrigin or AtTarget. Direct fitting needs it: model i pairs the target i steps ahead with the AtOrigin columns of the feature row and with the AtTarget columns of the target row, which is what forecast feeds. A TargetFeature is AtOrigin; a TimeFeature or an ExogenousFeature is AtTarget. A feature of any other kind must define it, otherwise an ArgumentError is thrown when Direct fits.\n\nAn AtOrigin feature may read only ctx.y: its training value comes from the feature row and its forecast value from the origin, while ctx.t, ctx.step and ctx.exog describe the context's row (the feature row in training, the target row when forecasting), so reading them would train on one thing and serve another. An AtTarget feature should not read ctx.y: under Direct it is trained on history up to the target row but served the history at the forecast origin. A feature that reads ctx.t, ctx.step or ctx.exog and not ctx.y is AtTarget: subtype TimeFeature or ExogenousFeature, or define anchor(f) = AtTarget(); one that reads both is correct only under Recursive.\n\nExample\n\nanchor(Lag(1))             # AtOrigin()\nanchor(Calendar(:month))   # AtTarget()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.anchor"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.anchors","page":"Home","text":"anchors(fs::FeatureSet) -> Vector{Anchor}\n\nThe anchor of every column of outputnames(fs), in the same order: AtOrigin columns describe the target's own history and belong to the feature row, AtTarget columns (time, exogenous) belong to the target row. Direct fitting shifts the two groups independently, see _train_models in src/forecaster.jl.\n\nExample\n\nanchors(FeatureSet(Lag(1), Calendar(:month, :year)))\n# Anchor[AtOrigin(), AtTarget(), AtTarget()]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.anchors"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Lag","page":"Home","text":"Lag(k)\n\nLagged target y_{t-k} as feature column y_lag_k. Requires a whole number k ≥ 1 (a Bool is refused).\n\nUnder Direct, Lag(k) counts back from the forecast origin, so at horizon h it is y[t0-k+1] (t0 is the index of the last observed value), not y[target-k]; Lag(m) is in phase with the target only when h = 1 (mod m); use Lag.(1:m), which holds an in-phase lag at every h, or Recursive.\n\nExample\n\nLag(7)   # the target seven steps ago, column :y_lag_7\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Lag"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RollingMean","page":"Home","text":"RollingMean(window; lag=1)\n\nRolling statistic of the target over window values ending lag steps before the current row, as column y_rollmean_<window>_lag_<lag>. The shift lag is applied before the window, so with the default lag=1 the window at row t covers y[t-lag-window+1 : t-lag] and never includes y[t] (no leakage). Requires window ≥ 1 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nRollingMean(7)        # mean of the 7 values before the current row\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RollingMean"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RollingStd","page":"Home","text":"RollingStd(window; lag=1)\n\nRolling statistic of the target over window values ending lag steps before the current row, as column y_rollstd_<window>_lag_<lag>. The shift lag is applied before the window, so with the default lag=1 the window at row t covers y[t-lag-window+1 : t-lag] and never includes y[t] (no leakage). Requires window ≥ 2 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nRollingStd(28)        # std of the 28 values before the current row\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RollingStd"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RollingMin","page":"Home","text":"RollingMin(window; lag=1)\n\nRolling statistic of the target over window values ending lag steps before the current row, as column y_rollmin_<window>_lag_<lag>. The shift lag is applied before the window, so with the default lag=1 the window at row t covers y[t-lag-window+1 : t-lag] and never includes y[t] (no leakage). Requires window ≥ 1 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nRollingMin(14)        # min of the 14 values before the current row\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RollingMin"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RollingMax","page":"Home","text":"RollingMax(window; lag=1)\n\nRolling statistic of the target over window values ending lag steps before the current row, as column y_rollmax_<window>_lag_<lag>. The shift lag is applied before the window, so with the default lag=1 the window at row t covers y[t-lag-window+1 : t-lag] and never includes y[t] (no leakage). Requires window ≥ 1 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nRollingMax(14)        # max of the 14 values before the current row\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RollingMax"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Diff","page":"Home","text":"Diff(k; lag=1)\n\nLagged difference of the target, y_{t-lag} - y_{t-lag-k}, as column y_diff_<k>_lag_<lag>. Requires k ≥ 1 and lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nDiff(1)       # first difference of yesterday: y_{t-1} - y_{t-2}\nDiff(7)       # week-over-week change as of yesterday: y_{t-1} - y_{t-8}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Diff"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Calendar","page":"Home","text":"Calendar(parts::Symbol...)\n\nCalendar features extracted from the time column, one column per part. Valid parts: :year, :quarter, :month, :weekofyear, :dayofweek, :dayofmonth, :dayofyear, :hour, :minute.\n\nExample\n\nCalendar(:dayofweek, :month)   # columns :dayofweek and :month\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Calendar"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Fourier","page":"Home","text":"Fourier(period, order)\n\nFourier seasonal terms sin(2πkn/period), cos(2πkn/period) for k = 1:order, where n is an integer step index (0-based from the first (transformed) training timestamp, incremented by 1 per freq step). The index continues seamlessly into the forecast horizon, so there is no phase jump at the train/forecast boundary. In a panel, n counts from the earliest usable series' first (transformed) training timestamp, so every series has the same Fourier values on the same date.\n\nperiod is expressed in units of freq steps, e.g. 365.25 for annual seasonality with daily data. order K produces 2K columns (fourier_<period>_sin_1..K, then fourier_<period>_cos_1..K).\n\nExample\n\nFourier(365.25, 3)   # annual seasonality, 3 harmonics, for daily data\nFourier(7, 2)        # weekly seasonality, 2 harmonics, for daily data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Fourier"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Exogenous","page":"Home","text":"Exogenous(cols::Symbol...)\n\nPass-through of user-supplied covariate columns that are known in the future (e.g. promotions, prices, holidays). At forecast time these columns must be supplied for the full horizon via the future keyword; in backtest they are sliced from the held-out data automatically. Exogenous columns must be numeric (Real or Bool). missing and ±Inf throw an ArgumentError.\n\nNaN is passed to the model unchanged. Learners built for missing values read it as missing: sparsity-aware trees learn a default direction for it (Chen & Guestrin 2016, KDD, §3.4), as does \"missing incorporated in attributes\" (Twala, Jones & Hand 2008, Pattern Recognition Letters 29(7), 950-956), which Josse et al. (2019, arXiv:1902.06931) recommend over imputation for trees. XGBoost, the system of the first citation, is one such learner. Learners without missing-value handling may treat NaN as an extreme value or refuse it; impute for those.\n\nA backtest uses the realised values of the covariates, so it scores ex-post forecasts (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos 2021, §7.6). For covariates not known at the origin (a competitor's price, the weather) this typically understates the error of the real, ex-ante forecast, which also carries the error of forecasting the covariate. For covariates known in advance (calendar effects, planned promotions, prices you set) ex-ante and ex-post agree. To measure ex-ante error, supply in the held-out rows the covariate values that were available at the origin (a forecast, or a lagged column).\n\nExample\n\nExogenous(:promo, :price)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Exogenous"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.CustomFeature","page":"Home","text":"CustomFeature(name, f, minhistory)\n\nA user-defined target-history feature. f is a function of the target history vector up to and excluding the current row, returning one finite real number (the value of the feature for that row). NaN and ±Inf throw an ArgumentError. minhistory is the minimum number of history values f needs; rows with less history get missing during training and are dropped.\n\nDuring recursive forecasting, f is called on the growing history vector (actuals followed by earlier predictions), so it sees exactly the same kind of input as during training.\n\nA function that reads anything but its argument, such as a captured series or parent(h), bypasses the leakage guarantee: f must read only the history it is given.\n\nIn training f is called once per row on the history before it, so a function that loops over the whole history makes fit O(n^2) in the series length; keep it cheap or use a built-in rolling feature.\n\nExample\n\nusing Statistics\n# mean of the entire history so far (needs at least 1 observation)\nCustomFeature(:hist_mean, mean, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.CustomFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.AbstractFeature","page":"Home","text":"AbstractFeature\n\nRoot of the feature hierarchy. Concrete features are one of:\n\nTargetFeature: computed from the target's history (updated during recursive forecasting), e.g. Lag, RollingMean.\nTimeFeature: computed from the timestamp only (always computable for future rows), e.g. Calendar, Fourier.\nExogenousFeature: passed through from user data (must be provided for the forecast as future), i.e. Exogenous.\n\nEvery concrete feature implements three methods:\n\noutputnames(f): the names of the columns it produces, a Vector{Symbol}.\nminhistory(f): the number of history rows it needs before it is defined.\nfeaturevalues!(out, f, ctx): writes its values for the one row that the FeatureContext ctx describes.\n\nTraining, forecast and backtest all compute a feature through featurevalues!, so by default (unless the feature overrides materialize!) the training columns and the forecast-time values cannot disagree. Optionally a feature defines materialize! (a faster way to fill its training columns), inputcolumns (the user columns it reads; an ExogenousFeature must) and anchor (which row its columns describe under Direct; a feature that subtypes AbstractFeature directly must).\n\nctx.y holds the target history strictly before the row's target: a TargetFeature never sees the current or a later target value, so rolling and difference features require lag ≥ 1. Under Direct the history is fixed at the forecast origin. An AtOrigin feature (every TargetFeature by default) should read only ctx.y: under Direct its training column comes from the feature row, while ctx.t and ctx.step describe the feature row in training and the target row when forecasting. See anchor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.AbstractFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TargetFeature","page":"Home","text":"TargetFeature\n\nAbstract supertype of the features computed from the target's history (updated during recursive forecasting), such as Lag and RollingMean. Subtype it for your own, then implement the methods listed under AbstractFeature. Its anchor is AtOrigin by default.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: outputnames, minhistory, featurevalues!\n\nstruct LastValue <: TargetFeature end\noutputnames(::LastValue) = [:last_value]\nminhistory(::LastValue) = 1\nfeaturevalues!(out, ::LastValue, ctx) = (out[1] = ctx.y[end]; nothing)\n\nFeatureSet(LastValue(), Lag(7))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TargetFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TimeFeature","page":"Home","text":"TimeFeature\n\nAbstract supertype of the features computed from the timestamp only, so always computable for future rows, such as Calendar and Fourier. Subtype it for your own, then implement the methods listed under AbstractFeature. Its anchor is AtTarget by default.\n\nExample\n\nusing Dates\nimport MachineLearningForecast: outputnames, featurevalues!\n\nstruct IsWeekend <: TimeFeature end\noutputnames(::IsWeekend) = [:is_weekend]\nfeaturevalues!(out, ::IsWeekend, ctx) = (out[1] = Float64(dayofweek(ctx.t) >= 6); nothing)\n\nFeatureSet(Lag(1), IsWeekend())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TimeFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.ExogenousFeature","page":"Home","text":"ExogenousFeature\n\nAbstract supertype of the features passed through from the user's columns, which must be passed to forecast as future, such as Exogenous. Subtype it for your own, implement the methods listed under AbstractFeature, and declare the columns it reads with inputcolumns. Its anchor is AtTarget by default. A feature that reads ctx.exog itself must check its own values: only Exogenous rejects ±Inf.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: outputnames, inputcolumns, featurevalues!\n\nstruct DoubledPromo <: ExogenousFeature end\noutputnames(::DoubledPromo) = [:promo2]\ninputcolumns(::DoubledPromo) = [:promo]\nfeaturevalues!(out, ::DoubledPromo, ctx) = (out[1] = 2.0 * ctx.exog.promo; nothing)\n\nFeatureSet(Lag(1), DoubledPromo())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.ExogenousFeature"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TargetTransform","page":"Home","text":"TargetTransform\n\nRoot of the per-series target transforms. A transform maps one series' training target y to the series z that the model learns and forecasts, and maps the forecasts of z back. Its state is fitted on that series' own training history only, so it never sees the forecast window, and backtest refits it in every fold.\n\nA transform implements two methods and may declare a third, public but not exported, so import them first:\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: fit_transform, untransform, dropped_rows\n\nfit_transform(t, y)        # -> (z, state); z may be shorter, aligned to y's end\nuntransform(t, ẑ, state)   # -> the forecasts of y for the forecasts ẑ of z\ndropped_rows(t)            # -> the rows z is shorter by (optional, default 0)\n\nFeatures are computed on z, and the first length(y) - length(z) training rows are dropped. Pass a transform as Forecaster(...; transform=...).\n\nExample\n\nfc = Forecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag.(1:12)...), freq=Month(1),\n                transform=TransformChain(LastScale(12), Deseasonalize(12),\n                                         LogRatio(12)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TargetTransform"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.fit_transform","page":"Home","text":"fit_transform(t::TargetTransform, y::AbstractVector{Float64}) -> (z, state)\n\nFit transform t on one series' training target y and return the transformed series z (possibly shorter, aligned to the end of y) and the state that untransform needs. Part of the TargetTransform contract. It is not exported, so import it: import MachineLearningForecast: fit_transform.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: fit_transform\n\nz, state = fit_transform(LastScale(3), [2.0, 4.0, 6.0, 8.0])   # z = y ./ 6\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.fit_transform"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.untransform","page":"Home","text":"untransform(t::TargetTransform, ẑ::AbstractVector{Float64}, state) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nMap the forecasts ẑ of the transformed series back to the original scale, step 1 first. Part of the TargetTransform contract. It is not exported, so import it: import MachineLearningForecast: fit_transform, untransform.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: fit_transform, untransform\n\nz, state = fit_transform(LastScale(3), [2.0, 4.0, 6.0, 8.0])\nuntransform(LastScale(3), [1.5, 1.5], state)   # [9.0, 9.0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.untransform"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.dropped_rows","page":"Home","text":"dropped_rows(t::TargetTransform) -> Int\n\nHow many rows fit_transform(t, y) removes from the start of a series: z has length(y) - dropped_rows(t) values when y is longer than that. An optional part of the TargetTransform contract, 0 by default. Declare it when your transform shortens the series. fit then checks each series, as the user gave it, against the feature set's history rows plus dropped_rows(t) before it calls fit_transform, so fit_transform never sees a series that is about to be skipped, and a panel skips its short series instead of failing on them. A transform that drops rows without declaring them is checked after it ran, as before. It is not exported, so import it: import MachineLearningForecast: dropped_rows.\n\nDifference(l) drops l rows and LogRatio(w) drops w, as lag-m differencing loses its first m observations; a chain drops the sum of its parts (Box, Jenkins, Reinsel and Ljung 2015, ch. 4).\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: dropped_rows\n\ndropped_rows(Difference(12))                                  # 12\ndropped_rows(TransformChain(Difference(1), Difference(12)))   # 13\ndropped_rows(LastScale(3))                                    # 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.dropped_rows"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.NoTransform","page":"Home","text":"NoTransform()\n\nThe identity transform, and the default: the model learns the target as given.\n\nExample\n\nForecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), freq=Day(1), transform=NoTransform())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.NoTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.MeanScale","page":"Home","text":"MeanScale()\n\nDivide each series by the mean of its whole training history, and multiply the forecasts back. Puts series of different levels on one scale for a global model.\n\nExample\n\nForecaster(model; features=FeatureSet(Lag(1)), freq=Day(1), id=:unique_id,\n           transform=MeanScale())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.MeanScale"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.LastScale","page":"Home","text":"LastScale(k)\n\nDivide each series by the mean of its last k training values (fewer if the series is shorter), and multiply the forecasts back. Unlike MeanScale it scales by the recent level, so a trending series starts its forecast near 1, inside the range a tree model has seen. Requires k ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nLastScale(12)   # monthly data: scale by the last year's mean\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.LastScale"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Deseasonalize","page":"Home","text":"Deseasonalize(period)\n\nClassical multiplicative seasonal adjustment, applied only to series that pass the M4 competition's seasonality test: the autocorrelation at lag period must exceed its 90% bound, which needs at least 3 * period values. A seasonal series is divided by seasonal indices: centred moving-average ratios averaged by position in the season and normalised to mean 1. The forecasts are multiplied by the index of their own position. Needs positive values when a series is adjusted, and period ≥ 2.\n\nExample\n\nDeseasonalize(12)   # monthly data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Deseasonalize"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.LogRatio","page":"Home","text":"LogRatio(window)\n\nModel each value as the log of its ratio to the mean of the window values before it, z_t = log(x_t / mean(x[t-window:t-1])), which is scale-free and keeps trending series stationary. The first window values have no ratio and are dropped. Forecasts are rebuilt step by step from the last window training values. Needs positive values and window ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nLogRatio(12)   # monthly data: ratio to the previous year's mean\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.LogRatio"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Difference","page":"Home","text":"Difference(lag)\n\nModel each value's change from lag steps before, z_t = y_t - y_{t-lag}, and rebuild forecasts by adding the changes back. Difference(1) removes a trend, Difference(12) a monthly season. The first lag values are dropped. Requires lag ≥ 1.\n\nExample\n\nTransformChain(Difference(1), Difference(12))   # lag 1 first, then lag 12\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Difference"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TransformChain","page":"Home","text":"TransformChain(transforms...)\n\nApply several transforms in order, each to the output of the one before, and undo them in reverse order. Needs at least one transform.\n\nExample\n\nTransformChain(LastScale(12), Deseasonalize(12), LogRatio(12))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TransformChain"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.backtest","page":"Home","text":"backtest(fc::Forecaster, data; horizon, initial=nothing, windows=nothing,\n         step=horizon, metrics=(mae, rmse)) -> BacktestResult\n\nExpanding-window time-series cross-validation. Pass exactly one of:\n\ninitial: train on rows 1:initial, forecast horizon steps, evaluate against the held-out actuals; advance the origin by step rows and repeat while a full horizon of actuals remains.\nwindows: hold out the last windows folds, step apart, the last ending at the end of the data. For a single series this is initial chosen so the final fold ends at the last row.\n\nFor a panel forecaster (one with id set), initial cuts folds on the global timestamp grid, since rows are spread across series: initial, step and horizon count distinct timestamps. Each fold trains on every row at or before the origin timestamp, forecasts each series forward from its own last observed timestamp, and scores the result by joining forecasts to actuals on (id, time), so ragged series contribute wherever they have actuals. windows instead counts the folds back from each series' own end, as the M competitions hold out each series' last values: in fold k, every series trains on its rows up to its own cutoff, and one global model is fitted on all of them. If the series are not aligned in calendar time, the training set of a fold can contain values from after another series' cutoff, a known pitfall of global models (Hewamalage, Ackermann & Bergmeir 2023). With initial, all series are cut at the same timestamp, which avoids it. For a panel, the forecasts table carries the id column too. A fold does not score a series that has no rows up to the fold's origin, too few of them to train on (it is skipped with a warning), or no actuals after it, so the set of scored series can change from fold to fold.\n\nmetrics is a tuple, vector or NamedTuple of callables (y, ŷ) -> Float64 (e.g. mae, rmse, smape). Each is named by metric_name (:mae, :mase_12 for Mase(12)) in the scores table, or by its key in a NamedTuple, metrics=(error=mae, scaled=Mase(12)); the names must be distinct. Scaled metrics that also need the fold's training target — mase, rmsse, Mase, Rmsse, or your own, declared via metric_inputs — are called as metric(y, ŷ; y_train=...) with that fold's training window supplied automatically (on a panel, each series' own, so a scale never mixes series). Pass Mase(12), not the type Mase. Unscaled metrics pool the fold's rows; scaled metrics are per series, weighted by scored points; summarize gives the unweighted mean over folds. Scaled metrics follow Hyndman & Koehler (2006): each series' errors are divided by that series' own training-window naive error, then averaged over series (here weighted by scored points, our choice). Unscaled metrics are pooled across series, so on a panel they are dominated by the large-scale series; use a scaled metric to compare series of different scale.\n\nWhen the feature set includes Exogenous columns, they are sliced from the held-out data automatically (they are \"known\" in a backtest). A backtest uses the realised values of the covariates, so it scores ex-post forecasts (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos 2021, §7.6). For covariates not known at the origin (a competitor's price, the weather) this typically understates the error of the real, ex-ante forecast, which also carries the error of forecasting the covariate. For covariates known in advance (calendar effects, planned promotions, prices you set) ex-ante and ex-post agree. To measure ex-ante error, supply in the held-out rows the covariate values that were available at the origin (a forecast, or a lagged column).\n\nExample\n\nresults = backtest(fc, df; horizon=28, initial=730, step=28,\n                   metrics=(mae, rmse, smape, Mase(7)))\nsummarize(results)   # mean and std of each metric over folds\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.backtest"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.BacktestResult","page":"Home","text":"BacktestResult\n\nResult of backtest. Both fields are columntables (NamedTuples of vectors, Tables.jl-compatible):\n\nforecasts: one row per fold × horizon step, with columns :fold, :origin (the last training timestamp the row's forecast starts from), :step (1..horizon), the spec's time column, :y (actual), and :y_hat (forecast). A panel backtest adds the id column after :step.\nscores: one row per fold × metric (columns :fold, :origin, :metric, :value). A fold's :origin is missing when its series start their forecasts at different timestamps. summarize reduces it to the mean and standard deviation of each metric over the folds.\n\nExample\n\nresults = backtest(fc, df; horizon=28, initial=730)\nresults.forecasts.y_hat   # every forecast, fold by fold\nresults.scores            # the score of each fold and metric\nsummarize(results)        # mean and std of each metric over folds\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.BacktestResult"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.summarize","page":"Home","text":"summarize(r::BacktestResult) -> NamedTuple\n\nSummarize the per-fold scores of a backtest: one row per metric, in the order the metrics were given, with columns :metric (Symbol), :mean (the unweighted mean of the metric over folds), :std (the sample standard deviation over folds, with divisor n - 1) and :nfolds (Int). :mean and :std are Float64. With a single fold :std is NaN. A NaN or Inf score in any fold makes :mean (and :std) non-finite. With metrics=() every column is empty.\n\nstd is the spread of the per-fold scores. Expanding-window folds share their training data and, when step < horizon, their test rows, so it describes the variability across origins and is not a standard error of mean.\n\nExample\n\nr = backtest(fc, df; horizon=28, initial=730, metrics=(mae, rmse))\nsummarize(r).mean   # [mae, rmse] averaged over folds\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.summarize"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.mae","page":"Home","text":"mae(y, ŷ) -> Float64\n\nMean absolute error, mean(|y - ŷ|).\n\nExample\n\nmae([1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 4.0])   # 1.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.mae"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.rmse","page":"Home","text":"rmse(y, ŷ) -> Float64\n\nRoot mean squared error, sqrt(mean((y - ŷ)²)).\n\nExample\n\nrmse([1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 4.0])   # ≈ 1.581\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.rmse"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.mape","page":"Home","text":"mape(y, ŷ) -> Float64\n\nMean absolute percentage error, mean(|y - ŷ| / |y|), as a fraction (0.10 = 10%). If any y is zero, MAPE is undefined: a warning is emitted and Inf is returned — prefer smape or mase for data with zeros.\n\nExample\n\nmape([10.0, 20.0], [11.0, 18.0])   # 0.1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.mape"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.smape","page":"Home","text":"smape(y, ŷ) -> Float64\n\nSymmetric mean absolute percentage error, mean(2|y - ŷ| / (|y| + |ŷ|)), as a fraction in [0, 2]. A term with y = ŷ = 0 contributes 0.\n\nExample\n\nsmape([10.0, 20.0], [11.0, 18.0])   # ≈ 0.1003\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.smape"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.mase","page":"Home","text":"mase(y, ŷ; y_train, m=1) -> Float64\n\nMean absolute scaled error: mae(y, ŷ) divided by the in-sample MAE of the (seasonal) naive forecast with seasonality m on y_train (mean(|y_train[t] - y_train[t-m]|)). A value below 1 means the errors are smaller on average than the in-sample one-step (lag-m) naive errors (Hyndman & Koehler 2006); multi-step errors are naturally larger, so compare with a naive baseline backtested at the same horizon. On intermittent series (many zeros), MAE-based measures, including MASE, can favour forecasts close to zero; the M5 competition used a squared-error scaled measure (RMSSE) for this reason (Makridakis, Spiliotis & Assimakopoulos 2022); use rmsse for such series. If the naive error is zero (constant training series), a warning is emitted and Inf is returned. One series with a constant training window makes that fold's score, and so its mean over folds in summarize, Inf.\n\nExample\n\nmase([10.0, 12.0], [11.0, 11.0]; y_train=[1.0, 3.0, 1.0, 3.0])   # 0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.mase"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.rmsse","page":"Home","text":"rmsse(y, ŷ; y_train, m=1) -> Float64\n\nRoot mean squared scaled error: rmse(y, ŷ) divided by the root of the in-sample mean squared error of the (seasonal) naive forecast with seasonality m on y_train,\n\nrmse(y, ŷ) / sqrt(mean((y_train[t] - y_train[t-m])²))\n\nthe scale being taken over the whole training window. It is the RMSSE of the M5 competition (Makridakis, Spiliotis & Assimakopoulos 2022), except that M5 computes the scale only from the first non-zero value on, and its seasonal form for any m (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos 2021, §5.8); Hyndman & Koehler (2006) suggest the in-sample RMSE of the naive method as the denominator of a scaled RMSE. A value below 1 means the errors are smaller than the in-sample one-step (lag-m) naive errors; multi-step errors are naturally larger, so compare with a naive baseline backtested at the same horizon.\n\nOn a single series, RMSSE ranks methods exactly as rmse does, since the scale is the same for all of them (Koutsandreas et al. 2022); the scale matters when comparing series. Squared errors suit intermittent series (many zeros), where absolute errors favour forecasts close to zero (Makridakis, Spiliotis & Assimakopoulos 2022; Hewamalage, Ackermann & Bergmeir 2023). The price is that RMSSE is more sensitive to outliers than mase (Hyndman & Koehler 2006; Hewamalage, Ackermann & Bergmeir 2023). If the naive error is zero (constant training series), a warning is emitted and Inf is returned.\n\nIn backtest it is computed per series, aggregated across series weighted by scored points, then averaged over folds. This is not the dollar-weighted WRMSSE of M5. One series with a constant training window makes that fold's score, and so its mean over folds in summarize, Inf.\n\nExample\n\nrmsse([10.0, 14.0], [11.0, 11.0]; y_train=[1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 7.0])   # ≈ 1.035\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.rmsse"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Mase","page":"Home","text":"Mase(m=1)\n\nCallable form of mase with season length m, for backtest and tune: Mase(m)(y, ŷ; y_train) equals mase(y, ŷ; y_train, m). It is scored per series with that series' own training window, and named :mase for m=1 and :mase_12 for m=12 in the scores table.\n\nExample\n\nMase()([10.0, 12.0], [11.0, 11.0]; y_train=[1.0, 3.0, 1.0, 3.0, 1.0])   # 0.5\nbacktest(fc, df; horizon=12, windows=3, metrics=(mae, Mase(12)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Mase"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Rmsse","page":"Home","text":"Rmsse(m=1)\n\nCallable form of rmsse with season length m, for backtest and tune: Rmsse(m)(y, ŷ; y_train) equals rmsse(y, ŷ; y_train, m). It is scored per series with that series' own training window, and named :rmsse for m=1 and :rmsse_7 for m=7 in the scores table.\n\nExample\n\nRmsse()([10.0, 14.0], [11.0, 11.0]; y_train=[1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 7.0])   # ≈ 1.035\nbacktest(fc, df; horizon=7, windows=3, metrics=(rmse, Rmsse(7)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Rmsse"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.SeasonalNaive","page":"Home","text":"SeasonalNaive(period)\n\nAn MLJ Deterministic model that needs no training: it predicts its y_lag_<period> input, the target one season back. With FeatureSet(Lag(period)) and Recursive a Forecaster repeats the last observed value at each step's position in the season, the seasonal naive forecast, at every horizon: the fed-back forecasts supply the lags beyond one season. This holds without a target transform or with a scaling one such as MeanScale; Difference or LogRatio change the forecasts. Use it as a baseline in backtest, or as the centre of ConformalSeasonalPool. Under Direct, Lag(period) is not in phase with the target at every step, so the forecasts are not seasonal naive: use Recursive().\n\nExample\n\nfc = Forecaster(SeasonalNaive(12); features=FeatureSet(Lag(12)), freq=Month(1))\nsummarize(backtest(fc, df; horizon=12, windows=3, metrics=(mae, smape)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.SeasonalNaive"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.metric_inputs","page":"Home","text":"metric_inputs(metric) -> MetricInput\n\nWhat metric is called with, so backtest and tune know how to score it: Point (the default) for metric(y, ŷ), Scaled for metric(y, ŷ; y_train), which also needs the fold's training target, and Samples for metric(y, S), which scores sampled forecasts (tune refuses it). Scaled for mase, rmsse, Mase and Rmsse. Define it for your own scaled metrics. Public, not exported.\n\nExample\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast\nimport MachineLearningForecast: metric_inputs, Scaled\nmy_mase(y, ŷ; y_train) = mae(y, ŷ) / mae(y_train[2:end], y_train[1:end-1])\nmetric_inputs(::typeof(my_mase)) = Scaled()\nbacktest(fc, df; horizon=12, windows=3, metrics=(mae, my_mase))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.metric_inputs"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.metric_name","page":"Home","text":"metric_name(metric) -> Symbol\n\nThe name of metric in the scores table of a BacktestResult: Symbol(metric) by default (:mae for mae), :mase or :mase_12 for Mase. Two metrics of one backtest need different names. Define it for a metric object of your own, or name a metric in place with a NamedTuple key: metrics=(error=mae,). Public, not exported.\n\nExample\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast\nimport MachineLearningForecast: metric_inputs, metric_name, Scaled\nstruct MyScaled\n    k::Int\nend\n(s::MyScaled)(y, ŷ; y_train) = mae(y, ŷ) / mae(y_train[2:end], y_train[1:end-1])\nmetric_inputs(::MyScaled) = Scaled()\nmetric_name(s::MyScaled) = Symbol(\"my_scaled_\", s.k)\nmetric_name(MyScaled(2))   # :my_scaled_2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.metric_name"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.MetricInput","page":"Home","text":"MetricInput\n\nSupertype of the kinds of inputs a metric takes, returned by metric_inputs: Point, Scaled and Samples. Public, not exported.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: metric_inputs, MetricInput\nmetric_inputs(mae) isa MetricInput   # true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.MetricInput"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Point","page":"Home","text":"Point()\n\nThe metric_inputs kind of a metric called as metric(y, ŷ), such as mae. backtest pools the fold's rows of all series. The default. Public, not exported.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: metric_inputs, Point\nmetric_inputs(mae) === Point()   # true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Point"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Scaled","page":"Home","text":"Scaled()\n\nThe metric_inputs kind of a metric called as metric(y, ŷ; y_train), such as mase. backtest calls it once per series with that series' own training values, and weights the series by their scored points. Public, not exported.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: metric_inputs, Scaled\nmetric_inputs(mase) === Scaled()   # true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Scaled"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.ConformalSeasonalPool","page":"Home","text":"ConformalSeasonalPool(period; freq, weight=AdaptiveWeight(), calibration=0.5,\n                      recency=0.01, samples=100, rng=Random.Xoshiro(42),\n                      target=:y, time=:ds, id=nothing) -> Forecaster\n\nThe forecaster of Conformal Seasonal Pools (CSP; Manokhin 2026, arXiv:2605.03789): a training-free probabilistic forecast around the seasonal naive forecast with season length period (in steps of freq; 1 for none). It returns a Forecaster with SeasonalNaive(period), FeatureSet(Lag(period)), Recursive(), no transform and intervals=PoolIntervals(period; weight, calibration, recency, samples, rng), the only setup the method is defined for (a hand-built Forecaster with a mismatched setup, or with features beyond FeatureSet(Lag(period)), is refused). With id set, each series of the panel has its own pools. weight is AdaptiveWeight() (the paper's CSP-Adaptive, the default) or FixedWeight(w) (FixedWeight(0.5) is CSP-Fixed); both, like PoolIntervals, are public and not exported: using MachineLearningForecast: PoolIntervals, FixedWeight. calibration, recency, samples and rng are described under PoolIntervals.\n\nThe method has no coverage guarantee: the paper reports empirical coverage only (mean 0.89 at the 0.95 level on its benchmark), so measure it on your data with Coverage in a backtest. Horizons are sampled independently; the samples are not paths.\n\nExample\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast, Dates\ncsp = ConformalSeasonalPool(12; freq=Month(1))\nfitted = fit(csp, df)      # trains nothing; keeps each series' history\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.ConformalSeasonalPool"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.PoolIntervals","page":"Home","text":"PoolIntervals(period; weight=AdaptiveWeight(), calibration=0.5, recency=0.01,\n              samples=100, rng=Random.Xoshiro(42))\n\nThe interval method of Conformal Seasonal Pools (CSP; Manokhin 2026, arXiv:2605.03789), the value of the intervals field of a Forecaster. For each series and horizon it draws samples values from a mixture: with probability w_h (weight), a past value from the same position in the season, recent ones more likely; otherwise the point forecast plus a signed seasonal difference y[t] - y[t - period] drawn uniformly from the last calibration share of the series. The defaults are the paper's. period is the season length in steps of freq (1 for none). Build the paired forecaster with ConformalSeasonalPool; a hand-built Forecaster accepts this method only with a SeasonalNaive(period) model, exactly FeatureSet(Lag(period)), Recursive() and no transform.\n\nThe seasonal pool weighs the value at time t by exp(-recency * (T - t)), with T - t in time steps of freq (so the weights fall by exp(-recency * period) per season). The paper writes the rate in \"normalized time-index units\" (App. A, p. 13), which is ambiguous; per step is our reading. recency=0 weighs the pool equally. When a horizon's season has no history, w_h is 0 and only residuals are drawn (our choice; the paper does not define it).\n\nrng is copied once per series at every call, so repeated calls give the same draws on the same Julia version (use StableRNGs.StableRNG for draws that also reproduce across versions) and the method's own rng is never advanced.\n\nThe method has no coverage guarantee: its residual pool is motivated by split-conformal calibration (Papadopoulos et al. 2002), whose finite-sample guarantee needs exchangeable data that time series are not, and the paper reports coverage as an empirical property (mean 0.89 at the 0.95 level on its benchmark). Measure it with Coverage in a backtest. Horizons are drawn independently, so a sample number does not trace a path; the residual pool is one pool for all horizons and is not scaled by the series; the paper did not test other values of samples, recency or calibration.\n\nExample\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast: PoolIntervals\nPoolIntervals(12; calibration=0.5, recency=0.01, samples=100)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.PoolIntervals"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.AdaptiveWeight","page":"Home","text":"AdaptiveWeight()\n\nThe paper's CSP-Adaptive rule for the seasonal-pool weight: 0 without seasonality (period = 1), 0.3 when one or two same-season values exist, 0.5 from three on. With no same-season value the weight is 0 (see PoolIntervals). The default of ConformalSeasonalPool.\n\nExample\n\nConformalSeasonalPool(12; freq=Month(1), weight=AdaptiveWeight())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.AdaptiveWeight"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.FixedWeight","page":"Home","text":"FixedWeight(w=0.5)\n\nA constant seasonal-pool weight w ∈ [0, 1] at every horizon. FixedWeight(0.5) is the paper's CSP-Fixed; FixedWeight(0.0) draws only residuals around the centre and FixedWeight(1.0) only past values from the same season (residuals when a horizon's season has no history).\n\nExample\n\nusing MachineLearningForecast: FixedWeight\nConformalSeasonalPool(12; freq=Month(1), weight=FixedWeight(0.5))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.FixedWeight"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.MixtureWeight","page":"Home","text":"MixtureWeight\n\nHow PoolIntervals weighs the seasonal pool against the residual pool at each horizon: AdaptiveWeight (the paper's CSP-Adaptive) or FixedWeight (CSP-Fixed). Public, not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.MixtureWeight"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.crps","page":"Home","text":"crps(y, S) -> Float64\n\nContinuous ranked probability score of sampled forecasts, averaged over the rows: for the actual y[i] and the samples x in row i of S, the CRPS of their empirical distribution, mean(|x - y|) - mean(|x - x'|) / 2 over all B^2 pairs (Gneiting and Raftery 2007). This plug-in estimator scores the B samples as issued (our choice of default; Zamo and Naveau 2018 recommend it for verifying the issued forecast); to score the distribution they are drawn from, or to compare different B, use fair_crps. In the units of y, lower is better; one sample gives the absolute error. The order of the samples does not matter.\n\nExample\n\ncrps([1.0], [0.0 2.0])   # 0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.crps"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.fair_crps","page":"Home","text":"fair_crps(y, S) -> Float64\n\nThe fair (ensemble-size adjusted) CRPS of sampled forecasts, averaged over the rows: like crps but with the spread term mean(|x - x'|) / 2 taken over the B (B - 1) pairs of distinct samples (Ferro, Richardson and Weigel 2008, eq. 12 with M = ∞). For samples drawn independently from the forecast distribution it is unbiased for that distribution's CRPS, so it compares forecasts with different numbers of samples; crps is higher by about E|X - X'| / (2B). It needs at least 2 samples per row.\n\nExample\n\nfair_crps([1.0], [0.0 2.0])   # 0.0; crps gives 0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.fair_crps"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.pinball_loss","page":"Home","text":"pinball_loss(y, S; quantiles=0.1:0.1:0.9) -> Float64\n\nMean pinball (quantile) loss of sampled forecasts: for every row and every τ in quantiles, u * (τ - (u < 0)) with u = y[i] - q, where q is the τ-quantile of row i of S (Hyndman and Fan type 6, our choice: a new draw falls below the k-th smallest sample with probability k / (B + 1)). It is the plain mean over rows and levels, not normalised (our choice). In the units of y, lower is better.\n\nExample\n\npinball_loss([1.0], zeros(1, 10))   # 0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.pinball_loss"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Coverage","page":"Home","text":"Coverage(level=95)\n\nMetric: the share of actuals inside the central level % interval of their samples, the interval between the sample quantiles at (100 - level) / 200 and (100 + level) / 200 (Hyndman and Fan type 6; a level above 100 (B - 1) / (B + 1) clamps to the sample range). Callable as Coverage(95)(y, S); named :coverage_95 in the scores table of a backtest. A calibrated forecast scores close to level / 100; higher is not better, so it is not a score to minimise. level is a percentage in (1, 100), so Coverage(0.95) is an error.\n\nExample\n\nCoverage(95)([50.0], reshape(collect(1.0:100.0), 1, 100))   # 1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Coverage"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.IntervalWidth","page":"Home","text":"IntervalWidth(level=95)\n\nMetric: the mean width of the central level % interval of the samples (the same interval as Coverage), a measure of sharpness. Callable as IntervalWidth(95)(y, S); named :interval_width_95. Compare widths only between forecasts with similar Coverage: a narrow interval that misses is not sharp, it is wrong.\n\nExample\n\nIntervalWidth(80)([1.0], [0.0 1.0 2.0])   # 2.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.IntervalWidth"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.Samples","page":"Home","text":"Samples()\n\nThe metric_inputs kind of a metric called as metric(y, S), where row i of the matrix S holds the sampled forecasts for y[i], such as crps. backtest does not score it yet, and tune refuses it. Public, not exported.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: metric_inputs, Samples\nmetric_inputs(crps) === Samples()   # true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.Samples"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.tune","page":"Home","text":"tune(fc::Forecaster, data; grid=nothing, tuner=GridSearch(), max_evals=nothing,\n     horizon, initial=nothing, windows=nothing, step=horizon,\n     metric=smape) -> TuneResult\n\nTune the whole forecasting pipeline on backtest score. Each candidate is a NamedTuple of Forecaster field overrides (e.g. model, features, strategy); the candidate spec is rebuilt from fc with those overrides, backtested with backtest(spec, data; horizon, initial, step, metrics=(score=metric,)), or with windows in place of initial, and scored by the mean of metric across folds (lower is better). metric is one point or scaled metric (metric_inputs), e.g. smape or Mase(7), checked before any fit. A metric that scores samples (crps, Coverage) is refused. Pass exactly one of initial and windows; windows counts each series' folds back from its own end (see backtest for the caveat when the series are not aligned in calendar time).\n\ngrid: NamedTuple mapping Forecaster field names (model, features, strategy, transform) to candidate value lists. Required for the batch strategies (GridSearch, the Cartesian product; RandomSearch, n uniform draws). A sequential user-defined TuningStrategy owns its own space and may ignore it. Before any fit, tune throws an ArgumentError if a batch candidate it will evaluate has a :strategy that is not a strategy, such as :recursive.\nmax_evals: optional evaluation budget; the loop stops after this many candidates even if the strategy proposes more.\nCandidate failures never abort the search: the error is caught, the score recorded as missing (and reported to the strategy via tell!), the message stored in the result table's :error column, and the candidate excluded from ranking. A non-finite mean score (NaN or Inf) counts as a failure. If every candidate fails, tune throws an ArgumentError listing the first five candidates with their overrides, the first error in full and the first line of each other error, then the number left out.\n\nReturns a TuneResult with the score table, the best spec, and the best spec refit on all of data. Ties are broken by first-seen order.\n\nExample\n\nusing EvoTrees\nresult = tune(fc, df;\n    grid = (model    = [EvoTreeRegressor(eta=0.05), EvoTreeRegressor(eta=0.1)],\n            features = [FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7)),\n                        FeatureSet(Lag(1), Lag(7), Fourier(365.25, 3))]),\n    horizon=28, initial=730, step=28, metric=smape)\nresult.best_fitted   # ready to forecast\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.tune"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TuneResult","page":"Home","text":"TuneResult\n\nResult of tune:\n\ntable: a columntable (NamedTuple of vectors) with one row per evaluated candidate — the overridden parameters (one column per key), :mean_score and :std_score (the mean and standard deviation of the metric across backtest folds; std_score is missing with a single fold), and :error (the error message if the candidate failed, missing otherwise). Failed candidates are excluded from ranking.\nbest::Forecaster: the best candidate spec (NOT fitted).\nbest_fitted::FittedForecaster: the best spec refit on ALL of the data.\n\nExample\n\nresult = tune(fc, df; grid=(strategy=[Recursive(), Direct(28)],),\n              horizon=28, initial=730)\nresult.table.mean_score             # one score per candidate\nresult.best                         # the winning spec\nforecast(result.best_fitted, 28)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TuneResult"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.TuningStrategy","page":"Home","text":"TuningStrategy\n\nAbstract supertype of tuning search strategies for tune. Shipped strategies: GridSearch and RandomSearch.\n\ntune is an ask!/tell! loop: it asks the strategy for the next candidate, scores it with backtest and tells the strategy the score. The batch strategies serve a precomputed candidate list through this loop; a sequential strategy, such as a Bayesian optimizer defined outside this package, drives it directly.\n\nExtending with your own (sequential) strategy\n\nSubtype TuningStrategy and implement two methods; tune will drive them in an ask!/tell! loop. The two functions are public but not exported, so import them first: import MachineLearningForecast: ask!, tell!.\n\nask!(s::MyStrategy) -> Union{NamedTuple,Nothing} — return the next candidate: a NamedTuple of Forecaster field overrides (keys among :model, :features, :strategy, :transform), drawn from your search space. Return nothing to signal termination.\ntell!(s::MyStrategy, candidate, score) — receive the result of evaluating candidate: a Float64 backtest score (lower is better), or missing if the candidate failed to evaluate.\n\nTermination is signalled either by ask! returning nothing, or by the max_evals budget enforced by tune. Your strategy owns its search-space representation (construct it with whatever space description it needs).\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: ask!, tell!\n\nmutable struct FirstK <: TuningStrategy\n    candidates::Vector{NamedTuple}\n    i::Int\nend\nask!(s::FirstK) = s.i > length(s.candidates) ? nothing :\n                  (c = s.candidates[s.i]; s.i += 1; c)\ntell!(s::FirstK, candidate, score) = nothing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.TuningStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.GridSearch","page":"Home","text":"GridSearch()\n\nExhaustive search over the Cartesian product of the grid values passed to tune. Deterministic candidate order (first grid key varies fastest); ties in score are broken by first-seen order.\n\nExample\n\ntune(fc, df; grid=(model=[m1, m2], strategy=[Recursive(), Direct(28)]),\n     tuner=GridSearch(), horizon=28, initial=730)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.GridSearch"},{"category":"type","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.RandomSearch","page":"Home","text":"RandomSearch(n; rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nRandom search: n candidates drawn from the grid passed to tune, sampling each grid key independently and uniformly (with replacement, so duplicates are possible). Pass an explicit rng for reproducibility.\n\nExample\n\nusing Random\ntune(fc, df; grid=(model=models, features=featuresets),\n     tuner=RandomSearch(10; rng=Xoshiro(1)), horizon=28, initial=730)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.RandomSearch"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.ask!","page":"Home","text":"ask!(strategy) -> Union{NamedTuple, Nothing}\n\nAsk a TuningStrategy for its next candidate: a NamedTuple of Forecaster field overrides (keys among :model, :features, :strategy, :transform). Return nothing to signal that the search is finished. The name ends in ! because a strategy advances its own state on each call. Part of the public tuning-extension API: implement it for your own strategy subtypes; see TuningStrategy for the full contract. It is not exported, so import it: import MachineLearningForecast: ask!.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: ask!\n\nmutable struct FirstK <: TuningStrategy\n    candidates::Vector{NamedTuple}\n    i::Int\nend\nask!(s::FirstK) = s.i > length(s.candidates) ? nothing :\n                  (c = s.candidates[s.i]; s.i += 1; c)\n\ns = FirstK([(strategy=Recursive(),), (strategy=Direct(28),)], 1)\nask!(s)   # (strategy = Recursive(),)\nask!(s)   # (strategy = Direct(28),)\nask!(s)   # nothing: the search is finished\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.ask!"},{"category":"function","location":"#MachineLearningForecast.tell!","page":"Home","text":"tell!(strategy, candidate, score)\n\nReport an evaluation result back to a TuningStrategy: candidate is the NamedTuple previously returned by ask!, and score is the Float64 mean backtest metric (lower is better) or missing if the candidate failed to evaluate. Part of the public tuning-extension API: implement it for your own strategy subtypes; see TuningStrategy for the full contract. It is not exported, so import it: import MachineLearningForecast: ask!, tell!.\n\nExample\n\nimport MachineLearningForecast: ask!, tell!\n\nmutable struct Logged <: TuningStrategy\n    candidates::Vector{NamedTuple}\n    scores::Vector{Union{Missing,Float64}}\nend\nask!(s::Logged) = length(s.scores) < length(s.candidates) ?\n                  s.candidates[length(s.scores) + 1] : nothing\ntell!(s::Logged, candidate, score) = push!(s.scores, score)\n\ns = Logged([(strategy=Recursive(),), (strategy=Direct(28),)], [])\ntune(fc, df; tuner=s, horizon=28, initial=730)\ns.scores   # each candidate's mean backtest score, or missing if it failed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MachineLearningForecast.tell!"}]
}
